window.QUESTIONS = [{"id":1,"page":2,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"MOS structure","difficulty":"Easy","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"A","questionImage":"assets/questions/01-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/01-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 1\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · MOS structure · Easy\nThe source/drain junctions of a MOSFET side-diffuse under the gate by L on each side during fabrication.\nD\nThe effective channel length is:\nA. L − 2L\ndrawn D\nB. L + 2L\ndrawn D\nC. L − L\ndrawn D\nD. L + L\ndrawn D\n","summary":"Lateral diffusion shortens the channel from both ends.","steps":["The drawn gate length \\(L_{\\mathrm{drawn}}\\) is the lithographic distance. The electrically active channel is the gap between the source and drain junctions beneath that gate.","The source extends inward by \\(L_D\\), and the drain extends inward by another \\(L_D\\). Subtract both encroachments: \\(L_{\\mathrm{eff}}=L_{\\mathrm{drawn}}-L_D-L_D\\).","This effective length is the length used in the ideal channel-current factor \\(W/L\\). Shortening the channel increases that factor if width and bias stay fixed."],"prerequisites":["MOSFET cross-section: gate, source, drain and channel.","Difference between a drawn dimension and an electrical dimension."],"pitfall":"The junctions approach each other under the gate. Adding diffusion lengths has the wrong direction; subtracting only one forgets the other junction.","formula":"\\[L_{\\mathrm{eff}}=L_{\\mathrm{drawn}}-2L_D\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":2,"page":2,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"Bulk terminal","difficulty":"Easy","options":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":"D","questionImage":"assets/questions/02-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/02-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 2\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · Bulk terminal · Easy\nIn a CMOS circuit, to keep every source/drain junction diode reverse-biased, the NMOS p-substrate and the\nPMOS n-well are normally connected to:\nA. Substrate to the most positive supply (V ), n-well to the most negative supply (ground)\nDD\nB. Both to ground, the lowest potential in the circuit\nC. Both to V , the highest potential in the circuit\nDD\nD. Substrate to the most negative supply (ground), n-well to the most positive supply (V )\nDD\nE. Both left floating, so that the junctions carry no current\n","summary":"Tie the NMOS substrate low and the PMOS well high.","steps":["A p-n junction has its p-type side as the anode and its n-type side as the cathode. Keeping the p side at or below the n side prevents forward bias.","An NMOS has n-type source/drain regions in a p-type body. Holding that body at the lowest circuit potential keeps both body junctions non-forward-biased as the source and drain voltages move within the supply rails.","A PMOS has p-type source/drain regions in an n-well. Holding the n-well at \\(V_{DD}\\), the highest rail, gives the corresponding safe polarity. A floating body does not establish this condition and can change the threshold voltage."],"prerequisites":["Doping types and p-n diode polarity.","Conventional CMOS bulk connections.","Body bias affects threshold voltage."],"pitfall":"Use the p-side/n-side rule for each device, rather than assuming both bodies should use the same rail.","formula":null,"sourceNote":"At a source or drain exactly equal to its body potential, the junction is at zero bias rather than strictly reverse bias. The conventional rail connections prevent forward bias for signals within the rails."},{"id":3,"page":3,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"Small-signal parameters","difficulty":"Easy","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"C","questionImage":"assets/questions/03-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/03-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 3\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · Small-signal parameters · Easy\nFor a MOSFET in saturation (square law, channel-length modulation neglected), which expression is NOT a\nvalid form of g ?\nm\nA. μ C (W/L)·V\nn ox OV\nB. √(2μ C (W/L)·I )\nn ox D\nC. I /V\nD OV\nD. 2I /V\nD OV\n","summary":"The expression ID/VOV is missing a factor of two.","steps":["Define \\(K=\\mu_n C_{ox}W/L\\) and \\(V_{OV}=V_{GS}-V_{TH}\\). In square-law saturation, neglecting channel-length modulation, \\(I_D=\\tfrac12 K V_{OV}^2\\).","Transconductance is the local derivative at fixed drain and body bias: \\(g_m=\\partial I_D/\\partial V_{GS}=K V_{OV}\\), because \\(\\partial V_{OV}/\\partial V_{GS}=1\\).","Eliminating \\(K\\) gives \\(g_m=2I_D/V_{OV}\\); eliminating \\(V_{OV}\\) gives \\(g_m=\\sqrt{2K I_D}\\). Therefore A, B and D are equivalent, whereas C is only half of \\(g_m\\)."],"prerequisites":["Square-law saturation current.","Definition of small-signal transconductance.","Differentiation and substitution."],"pitfall":"The question asks which expression is NOT valid. A DC ratio \\(I_D/V_{OV}\\) is not the slope of the quadratic current curve.","formula":"\\[g_m=K V_{OV}=\\frac{2I_D}{V_{OV}}=\\sqrt{2KI_D}\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":4,"page":3,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"Source follower","difficulty":"Easy","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"B","questionImage":"assets/questions/04-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/04-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 4\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · Source follower · Easy\nWhich statement about the source follower (common-drain stage) with source resistor R is correct?\nS\nNeglect channel-length modulation (r → ∞) and the body effect.\no\nA. The gain is −g R , the output is inverted and R ≈ R\nm S out S\nB. The gain is g R /(1 + g R ) < 1 and R ≈ (1/g ) ∥ R\nm S m S out m S\nC. The gain is slightly above 1 and R ≈ r ∥ R\nout o S\nD. The input resistance is about 1/g and the gain is +g R\nm m S\n","summary":"A source follower is a non-inverting voltage buffer with gain below one.","steps":["The drain is at AC ground and the source is the output, so \\(v_{gs}=v_{in}-v_{out}\\). The source-resistor current is \\(v_{out}/R_S\\). KCL gives \\(g_m(v_{in}-v_{out})=v_{out}/R_S\\).","Rearrange to obtain \\(A_v=g_mR_S/(1+g_mR_S)\\). For positive \\(g_m\\) and \\(R_S\\), this is positive and less than one; it approaches one when \\(g_mR_S\\) is large.","To find output resistance, set the input signal to zero and apply a test voltage at the source. The transistor contributes conductance \\(g_m\\), and the resistor contributes \\(1/R_S\\), so \\(R_{out}=1/(g_m+1/R_S)\\). The ideal MOS gate draws no small-signal input current."],"prerequisites":["Small-signal MOS model.","KCL at the source node.","Output resistance with the input signal set to zero."],"pitfall":"The expression \\(-g_mR_D\\) belongs to a common-source stage. Here a rising output reduces \\(v_{gs}\\), providing local negative feedback.","formula":"\\[A_v=\\frac{g_mR_S}{1+g_mR_S},\\qquad R_{out}=\\frac{1}{g_m}\\parallel R_S\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":5,"page":4,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"Common-gate stage","difficulty":"Easy","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"B","questionImage":"assets/questions/05-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/05-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 5\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · Common-gate stage · Easy\nFor a common-gate stage with load R (gate at AC ground, body effect neglected, r ≫ R ), the input\nD o D\nresistance and the sign of the voltage gain are approximately:\nA. R → ∞, gain −g R\nin m D\nB. R ≈ 1/g , gain +g R\nin m m D\nC. R ≈ R , gain +g R\nin D m D\nD. R ≈ 1/g , gain −g R\nin m m D\n","summary":"The common-gate input is low resistance and its voltage gain is positive.","steps":["The gate is at AC ground while the input drives the source. Therefore \\(v_{gs}=-v_{in}\\). A positive source-voltage increment reduces the transistor drain-to-source current.","With \\(r_o\\) neglected, the current entering the source port is approximately \\(i_{in}=g_m v_{in}\\), so \\(R_{in}=v_{in}/i_{in}\\approx1/g_m\\).","The reduction in drain current reduces the drop across \\(R_D\\), raising the drain voltage. The small-signal output is \\(v_{out}\\approx g_mR_Dv_{in}\\), hence the stage is non-inverting."],"prerequisites":["MOS small-signal controlled current source.","AC ground and signal voltage polarity.","Voltage drop across a drain load."],"pitfall":"The MOS gate has high input resistance, but this circuit is driven at the source. Also distinguish the signed drain-current change from the current entering the source port.","formula":"\\[R_{in}\\approx\\frac{1}{g_m},\\qquad A_v\\approx+g_mR_D\\]","sourceNote":"The usual \\(1/g_m\\) approximation additionally assumes appreciable intrinsic gain \\(g_mr_o\\gg1\\). With finite \\(r_o\\), the exact resistance is \\((r_o+R_D)/(1+g_mr_o)\\), derived in Question 54."},{"id":6,"page":4,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"I–V curves","difficulty":"Easy","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"C","questionImage":"assets/questions/06-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/06-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 6\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · I–V curves · Easy\nThe figure shows the output characteristics of an NMOS with V = 0.4 V (square law, λ = 0); the dashed\nTH\ncurve is the saturation edge. At the marked point P (V = 1.0 V, V = 0.3 V) the transistor operates in:\nGS DS\nA. Cut-off, since V is below V\nDS TH\nB. Saturation, since V ≥ V − V\nDS GS TH\nC. Triode, since V < V − V\nDS GS TH\nD. Subthreshold, since I is below its maximum\nD\n","summary":"Point P is in triode because VDS is below the overdrive.","steps":["First check that the gate forms a channel: \\(V_{GS}=1.0\\,\\mathrm{V}>V_{TH}=0.4\\,\\mathrm{V}\\). The device is therefore above threshold.","Calculate \\(V_{OV}=V_{GS}-V_{TH}=0.6\\,\\mathrm{V}\\). Long-channel saturation requires \\(V_{DS}\\ge V_{OV}\\), but the point has only \\(V_{DS}=0.3\\,\\mathrm{V}\\).","Because the drain-end gate-to-channel voltage is still above threshold, the channel remains inverted from source to drain. The point lies left of the saturation boundary, in the rising triode section of the output curve."],"prerequisites":["Overdrive voltage.","Cutoff, triode and saturation conditions.","How to read an output-characteristic plot."],"pitfall":"Cutoff is decided by \\(V_{GS}\\) relative to \\(V_{TH}\\), not by comparing \\(V_{DS}\\) with \\(V_{TH}\\). A small drain current alone does not identify weak inversion.","formula":"\\[0<V_{DS}=0.3\\,\\mathrm{V}<V_{OV}=0.6\\,\\mathrm{V}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\text{triode}\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":7,"page":5,"lecture":2,"lectureName":"SPICE Modeling","topic":"DC operating point","difficulty":"Easy","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"A","questionImage":"assets/questions/07-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/07-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 7\nL2 SPICE Modeling · DC operating point · Easy\nDuring a SPICE DC operating-point analysis, how are capacitors and inductors treated?\nA. Capacitors as open circuits, inductors as short circuits\nB. Capacitors as short circuits, inductors as open circuits\nC. Both as open circuits\nD. Both by their impedance at 1 Hz\n","summary":"In a DC operating point, ideal capacitors are open and ideal inductors are short.","steps":["A DC operating point is a time-invariant steady state. For an ideal capacitor, \\(i_C=C\\,dv_C/dt\\); a constant voltage makes \\(dv_C/dt=0\\), so its current is zero.","For an ideal inductor, \\(v_L=L\\,di_L/dt\\); a constant current makes \\(di_L/dt=0\\), so its voltage is zero even though it may carry current.","The simulator solves the remaining nonlinear DC circuit to obtain the bias voltages and currents. These determine the transistor parameters used for ordinary small-signal AC analysis."],"prerequisites":["Ideal capacitor and inductor constitutive equations.","Meaning of DC steady state.","Role of the bias point in circuit simulation."],"pitfall":"Open circuit means zero current, not necessarily zero voltage. Short circuit means zero voltage, not necessarily zero current.","formula":"\\[i_C=C\\frac{dv_C}{dt}=0,\\qquad v_L=L\\frac{di_L}{dt}=0\\]","sourceNote":"This is the ideal-element DC rule. Real component models may contain series resistance or leakage. The bank's broad statement about every analysis is simplified: special transient initialization modes can bypass the usual operating-point solution."},{"id":8,"page":5,"lecture":2,"lectureName":"SPICE Modeling","topic":"AC analysis","difficulty":"Easy","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"D","questionImage":"assets/questions/08-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/08-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 8\nL2 SPICE Modeling · AC analysis · Easy\nWhich statement about SPICE AC analysis is correct?\nA. It captures the harmonic distortion of the transistors, because the full nonlinear models are\nevaluated at each frequency\nB. It does not require a DC operating point, since the capacitors and inductors set the response\nC. It integrates the circuit equations in time with the trapezoidal rule and then takes a Fourier\ntransform\nD. It is a small-signal linear analysis around the DC operating point, so no nonlinear effects appear\n","summary":"AC analysis solves a linearized circuit around the DC bias point.","steps":["The nonlinear DC solution identifies each device's operating point. The simulator then evaluates local derivatives such as \\(g_m=\\partial I_D/\\partial V_{GS}\\) and \\(g_{ds}=\\partial I_D/\\partial V_{DS}\\), together with the relevant small-signal capacitances.","Each frequency uses complex impedances or admittances, such as \\(Y_C=j\\omega C\\). The resulting linear system returns sinusoidal magnitude and phase responses.","A linear model cannot create harmonics, clipping or intermodulation. Those large-signal nonlinear behaviors require an appropriate nonlinear analysis, often a transient simulation; an AC sweep is not a time integration followed by a Fourier transform."],"prerequisites":["DC operating-point calculation.","Linearization using local derivatives.","Complex impedance and frequency response."],"pitfall":"The original transistor model can be nonlinear while the AC calculation is linear. Bias-dependent parameters influence the response, but signal-amplitude distortion is absent.","formula":"\\[i_d\\approx g_m v_{gs}+g_{ds}v_{ds}\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":9,"page":6,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Semiconductor properties","difficulty":"Easy","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"B","questionImage":"assets/questions/09-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/09-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 9\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Semiconductor properties · Easy\nThe band-gap energy of silicon near room temperature is approximately:\nA. 0.66 eV\nB. 1.12 eV\nC. 5.47 eV\nD. 9 eV\n","summary":"Silicon has a band gap of approximately 1.12 eV near room temperature.","steps":["The band gap \\(E_g\\) is the energy separation between the valence-band edge and the conduction-band edge. It is a material property, expressed here in electronvolts.","For silicon near \\(300\\,\\mathrm{K}\\), \\(E_g\\approx1.12\\,\\mathrm{eV}\\). At that temperature \\(kT\\approx0.0259\\,\\mathrm{eV}\\), so the gap is roughly \\(43kT\\).","The other listed scales correspond approximately to germanium (0.66 eV), diamond (5.47 eV) and silicon dioxide (about 9 eV). A larger band gap generally suppresses thermally generated intrinsic carriers; the actual carrier density also depends on the densities of states."],"prerequisites":["Valence and conduction bands.","Energy units: eV versus volts.","Thermal energy kT."],"pitfall":"Do not confuse the silicon band gap with a MOS threshold voltage. Threshold voltage is a device-and-bias property, whereas the band gap is a semiconductor material property.","formula":"\\[E_g(\\mathrm{Si},300\\,\\mathrm{K})\\approx1.12\\,\\mathrm{eV}\\]","sourceNote":"The source's electron-fraction comment is only a rough illustration. Intrinsic carrier concentration depends on \\(n_i=\\sqrt{N_CN_V}\\exp[-E_g/(2kT)]\\); the band gap alone does not determine a universal fraction of conduction electrons."},{"id":10,"page":6,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Doping","difficulty":"Easy","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"C","questionImage":"assets/questions/10-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/10-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 10\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Doping · Easy\nDoping silicon with phosphorus (group V, five valence electrons) produces:\nA. p-type material, each P atom contributing a hole and leaving a fixed negative ion\nB. Intrinsic material, since each P atom creates one electron–hole pair\nC. n-type material, each P atom contributing a conduction electron and leaving a fixed positive ion\nD. An insulator, since the extra electrons fill the conduction band\n","summary":"Phosphorus is a donor: it supplies an electron and leaves a positive ion.","steps":["Silicon forms four covalent bonds. A substitutional phosphorus atom has five valence electrons: four participate in bonding and the extra electron occupies a shallow donor state.","At room temperature in the usual nondegenerate doping regime, that donor electron is readily ionized into the conduction band. Once it loses the electron, the phosphorus donor is a fixed positively charged ion.","The added mobile electrons make electrons the majority carriers, producing n-type silicon. This process creates a mobile-electron/fixed-donor-ion pair, not an electron-hole pair. A group-III acceptor such as boron instead produces p-type behavior."],"prerequisites":["Silicon covalent bonding.","Donor and acceptor dopants.","Mobile carriers versus fixed ionized impurities."],"pitfall":"The released electron is negative, but the donor left behind is positive. Doping changes majority-carrier concentration; it does not make silicon intrinsically neutral in carrier type.","formula":null,"sourceNote":null},{"id":11,"page":7,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"I–V derivation","difficulty":"Easy","options":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":"A","questionImage":"assets/questions/11-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/11-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 11\nL3 Transistor Impairments · I–V derivation · Easy\nIn the long-channel model, the minimum V for saturation (V ) is:\nDS D,sat\nA. V − V\nGS TH\nB. V\nTH\nC. V\nGS\nD. 2(V − V )\nGS TH\nE. V /2\nDD\n","summary":"Long-channel saturation begins when VDS reaches VGS minus VTH.","steps":["The channel potential rises from the source toward the drain. At a position \\(x\\), inversion requires the local gate-to-channel voltage \\(V_{GS}-V(x)\\) to exceed \\(V_{TH}\\).","The weakest inversion is at the drain, where \\(V(x)=V_{DS}\\). At the boundary, \\(V_{GS}-V_{DS}=V_{TH}\\), or equivalently \\(V_{GD}=V_{TH}\\).","Solving gives \\(V_{DS,\\mathrm{sat}}=V_{GS}-V_{TH}=V_{OV}\\). At larger drain bias, the ideal long-channel channel is pinched off near the drain and its current no longer increases with \\(V_{DS}\\) when channel-length modulation is neglected."],"prerequisites":["Channel potential along a MOSFET.","Inversion condition and overdrive.","Pinch-off in the long-channel model."],"pitfall":"This condition assumes the transistor is on, \\(V_{GS}>V_{TH}\\). It is not simply \\(V_{DS}=V_{TH}\\), and short-channel velocity saturation can alter the boundary.","formula":"\\[V_{DS,\\mathrm{sat}}=V_{GS}-V_{TH}=V_{OV}\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":12,"page":7,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Deep triode","difficulty":"Easy","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"D","questionImage":"assets/questions/12-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/12-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 12\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Deep triode · Easy\nAn NMOS driven into deep triode (V ≪ 2(V − V )) behaves as a resistor R between drain and source, as\nDS GS TH\nin the figure. R equals:\nA. μ C (W/L)(V − V )\nn ox GS TH\nB. (V − V )/I\nGS TH D\nC. 1/(λI )\nD\nD. 1 / [μ C (W/L)(V − V )]\nn ox GS TH\n","summary":"In deep triode, channel resistance is the reciprocal of K times overdrive.","steps":["The triode equation is \\(I_D=K[V_{OV}V_{DS}-V_{DS}^2/2]\\), with \\(K=\\mu_nC_{ox}W/L\\). The quadratic term is small when \\(V_{DS}/(2V_{OV})\\ll1\\).","Neglect that term to obtain \\(I_D\\approx K V_{OV}V_{DS}\\). This has Ohm's-law form \\(I_D=V_{DS}/R\\), so \\(R\\approx1/(K V_{OV})\\).","For \\(\\mu_nC_{ox}=50\\,\\mu\\mathrm{A}/\\mathrm{V}^2\\), \\(W/L=10\\), and \\(V_{OV}=1\\,\\mathrm{V}\\), the conductance is \\(500\\,\\mu\\mathrm{S}\\), giving \\(R=2\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega\\). More overdrive or more width reduces the on-resistance."],"prerequisites":["Triode current equation.","Small-voltage approximation.","Ohm's law and conductance/resistance units."],"pitfall":"Option A is conductance, not resistance. The expression \\(1/(\\lambda I_D)\\) describes saturation output resistance from channel-length modulation, not triode on-resistance.","formula":"\\[R_{on}\\approx\\frac{1}{\\mu_nC_{ox}(W/L)(V_{GS}-V_{TH})}\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":13,"page":8,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Short-channel effects","difficulty":"Easy","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"A","questionImage":"assets/questions/13-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/13-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 13\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Short-channel effects · Easy\nThe short-channel threshold roll-off that the lecture presents under drain-induced barrier lowering (DIBL)\nrefers to:\nA. A decrease of V at short L, caused by the source/drain depletion regions protruding into the\nT\nchannel\nB. An increase of V at short L, caused by the gate losing control of the channel\nT\nC. A decrease of mobility at high vertical field, caused by surface scattering\nD. Saturation of the carrier velocity at high lateral field, caused by phonon scattering\n","summary":"The stated short-channel roll-off lowers threshold voltage.","steps":["In a long-channel device, the gate largely controls the depletion charge below the channel. A certain gate voltage is required to deplete the surface and establish strong inversion.","When the channel becomes short, source and drain depletion regions extend into a larger fraction of that region. The junction fields support part of the depletion charge, so the gate needs less voltage to reach inversion.","This makes threshold voltage decrease as channel length shrinks in the scenario described. Mobility degradation from vertical field and velocity saturation from lateral field are different effects; neither is the definition requested here."],"prerequisites":["Depletion regions at source/body and drain/body junctions.","Gate electrostatic control of the channel.","Distinction between threshold, mobility and carrier velocity."],"pitfall":"Loss of gate control can increase off-state leakage while lowering threshold. It does not imply that a larger gate voltage is needed to turn the transistor on.","formula":null,"sourceNote":"The bank groups threshold roll-off under DIBL. More precisely, charge sharing explains a length-dependent threshold shift, while DIBL describes the additional reduction of the source-channel barrier as drain bias rises. The effects are related but distinct. <a href=\"https://hasler.ece.gatech.edu/Courses/ECE3400/Proj1/Paper_DIBL_detailed.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Primary paper on short-channel threshold shifts</a>."},{"id":14,"page":8,"lecture":4,"lectureName":"Performance Measures of a MOS","topic":"Intrinsic gain","difficulty":"Easy","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"B","questionImage":"assets/questions/14-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/14-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 14\nL4 Performance Measures of a MOS · Intrinsic gain · Easy\nWith channel-length modulation the transistor has a finite output resistance r . Its intrinsic gain, the\no\nmaximum voltage gain of a common-source stage (R → ∞), is:\nL\nA. g ·R\nm L\nB. g ·r\nm o\nC. g /I\nm D\nD. g /C\nm gs\n","summary":"Intrinsic gain is gmro, the unloaded gain magnitude of a common-source device.","steps":["A common-source stage has approximately \\(A_v=-g_m(r_o\\parallel R_L)\\). The negative sign denotes inversion; gain capability is usually quoted as a positive magnitude.","As the external load resistance tends to infinity, the drain is still loaded by the transistor's own finite \\(r_o\\). Thus the limiting magnitude is \\(g_mr_o=g_m/g_{ds}\\).","Using square-law \\(g_m=2I_D/V_{OV}\\) and \\(r_o\\approx1/(\\lambda I_D)\\), the current cancels: intrinsic gain is approximately \\(2/(\\lambda V_{OV})\\). Smaller channel-length modulation or lower overdrive raises this idealized gain."],"prerequisites":["Common-source small-signal voltage gain.","Output resistance from channel-length modulation.","Magnitude versus sign of gain."],"pitfall":"\\(g_m/I_D\\) measures transconductance efficiency, and \\(g_m/C_{gs}\\) measures a speed scale. Neither is a dimensionless voltage gain.","formula":"\\[|A_{v,\\mathrm{intrinsic}}|=g_mr_o=\\frac{g_m}{g_{ds}}\\approx\\frac{2}{\\lambda V_{OV}}\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":15,"page":9,"lecture":4,"lectureName":"Performance Measures of a MOS","topic":"Subthreshold swing","difficulty":"Easy","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"D","questionImage":"assets/questions/15-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/15-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 15\nL4 Performance Measures of a MOS · Subthreshold swing · Easy\nThe subthreshold swing (SS) of a MOSFET is:\nA. The change in I per volt of V in strong inversion, in A/V\nD GS\nB. The slope of I versus V in deep triode, i.e. 1/R\nD DS on\nC. The threshold-voltage shift per °C, about −2 mV/°C\nD. The change in V needed to change I by one decade in weak inversion, in mV/decade\nGS D\n","summary":"Subthreshold swing is the gate-voltage change needed for one decade of drain-current change.","steps":["In weak inversion, the drain current is approximately exponential in gate voltage: \\(I_D\\propto\\exp[V_{GS}/(nU_T)]\\), with thermal voltage \\(U_T=kT/q\\).","Taking a base-10 logarithm produces a straight-line relation: \\(d\\log_{10}I_D/dV_{GS}=1/(nU_T\\ln10)\\). Subthreshold swing is the inverse slope.","One decade means multiplying current by ten. At room temperature, \\(U_T\\ln10\\approx60\\,\\mathrm{mV}\\), giving \\(SS\\approx60n\\,\\mathrm{mV/decade}\\). A lower swing requires less gate-voltage change for a given on/off current ratio."],"prerequisites":["Exponential weak-inversion current.","Base-10 logarithms and decades.","Thermal voltage and the nonideality factor n."],"pitfall":"\\(dI_D/dV_{GS}\\) is transconductance in siemens. SS uses a logarithm of current and has units of voltage per decade.","formula":"\\[SS\\equiv\\frac{dV_{GS}}{d\\log_{10}I_D}=nU_T\\ln10\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":16,"page":9,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"Channel-length modulation","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":"C","questionImage":"assets/questions/16-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/16-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 16\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · Channel-length modulation · Medium\nWith channel-length modulation, I = I (1 + λV ). Which statement is correct?\nD D0 DS\nA. r ≈ λI and V = λ, so r rises with bias current\no D A o\nB. The slope dI /dV is the same for every bias current, so r is bias-independent\nD DS o\nC. r ≈ 1/(λI ) and the extrapolated saturation curves meet at V = −V = −1/λ\no D DS A\nD. The common intercept −V depends on V , so it cannot serve as a device parameter\nA GS\nE. λ increases with L, so long devices have lower gain\n","summary":"Channel-length modulation gives finite ro and a common negative Early-voltage intercept.","steps":["At a fixed gate bias, write \\(I_D=I_{D0}(1+\\lambda V_{DS})\\). Its drain-voltage slope is \\(g_{ds}=\\partial I_D/\\partial V_{DS}=\\lambda I_{D0}\\), so \\(r_o=1/(\\lambda I_{D0})\\).","Because \\(I_D\\approx I_{D0}\\) when \\(\\lambda V_{DS}\\) is small, a common approximation is \\(r_o\\approx1/(\\lambda I_D)\\). A higher bias current gives a larger slope and therefore a smaller resistance.","Extrapolating the straight saturation line to zero current gives \\(1+\\lambda V_{DS}=0\\), so \\(V_{DS}=-1/\\lambda=-V_A\\). In this simple model the intercept is independent of gate bias. Longer channels usually have smaller \\(\\lambda\\), and therefore larger Early voltage and output resistance at comparable current."],"prerequisites":["Slope resistance and differential conductance.","Channel-length modulation model.","Extrapolation to the Early-voltage intercept."],"pitfall":"\\(\\lambda I_D\\) has units of conductance, not resistance. Also distinguish the positive parameter \\(V_A\\) from its negative horizontal-axis intercept.","formula":"\\[r_o=\\frac{1}{\\lambda I_{D0}}=\\frac{1+\\lambda V_{DS}}{\\lambda I_D}\\approx\\frac{1}{\\lambda I_D},\\quad V_A=\\frac1\\lambda\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":17,"page":10,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"Common-source stage","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"B","questionImage":"assets/questions/17-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/17-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 17\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · Common-source stage · Medium\nIn the common-source stage with resistive load R shown in the figure, M1 leaves saturation and enters\nD\ntriode when V exceeds V (point P on the transfer curve). At that boundary:\nin in1\nA. V = V\nout in1\nB. V = V − V\nout in1 TH\nC. V = V\nout DD\nD. V = V\nout TH\n","summary":"At the saturation-to-triode boundary, Vout equals Vin1 minus VTH.","steps":["The source is grounded in this common-source circuit. Consequently \\(V_{GS}=V_{in}\\) and \\(V_{DS}=V_{out}\\).","The long-channel saturation boundary is \\(V_{DS}=V_{GS}-V_{TH}\\). Substituting the node voltages at point P gives \\(V_{out}=V_{in1}-V_{TH}\\).","As the input rises, drain current tends to rise and the resistor drop increases, pulling the output down. Eventually the output becomes too low to satisfy the saturation condition, so the device enters triode and the transfer characteristic becomes strongly nonlinear."],"prerequisites":["Grounded-source voltage relationships.","Long-channel operating-region boundary.","Common-source transfer characteristic and resistor load line."],"pitfall":"At the boundary the gate-to-drain voltage equals threshold. The gate and drain voltages themselves are not equal.","formula":"\\[V_{out}=V_{DS}=V_{in1}-V_{TH}\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":18,"page":10,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"Source follower","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"A","questionImage":"assets/questions/18-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/18-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 18\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · Source follower · Medium\nA source follower has g = 5 mS, r = 10 kΩ and R = 2 kΩ (body effect neglected). Its exact small-signal\nm o S\noutput resistance is:\nA. 179 Ω\nB. 200 Ω\nC. 1.67 kΩ\nD. 12.2 kΩ\n","summary":"All three output conductances add, giving about 179 ohms.","steps":["For output resistance, set the gate input signal to zero and treat the drain supply as AC ground. A test voltage at the source sees the transistor's \\(g_m\\) conductance, its output conductance \\(1/r_o\\), and the resistor conductance \\(1/R_S\\).","Here \\(g_m=5\\,\\mathrm{mS}\\), \\(1/r_o=1/(10\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega)=0.1\\,\\mathrm{mS}\\), and \\(1/R_S=1/(2\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega)=0.5\\,\\mathrm{mS}\\). The sum is \\(5.6\\,\\mathrm{mS}\\).","Taking the reciprocal gives \\(R_{out}=1/(5.6\\,\\mathrm{mS})\\approx178.6\\,\\Omega\\). This is lower than each of the three parallel resistances, as it must be."],"prerequisites":["Source-follower small-signal output resistance.","Parallel conductances.","Conversion between mS and ohms."],"pitfall":"\\(1/g_m=200\\,\\Omega\\) is a useful approximation, but this question asks for the exact value within the stated model. Ignoring \\(g_m\\) yields the incorrect \\(r_o\\parallel R_S\\).","formula":"\\[R_{out}=\\left(g_m+\\frac1{r_o}+\\frac1{R_S}\\right)^{-1}\\approx179\\,\\Omega\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":19,"page":11,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"Thévenin and Norton","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"C","questionImage":"assets/questions/19-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/19-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 19\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · Thévenin and Norton · Medium\nA one-port contains resistors, independent sources and a dependent (controlled) source. The correct way\nto find its Thévenin resistance R is:\nTH\nA. Zero every source, dependent ones included, and compute the resistance seen at the port\nB. Replace the dependent source by an open circuit and zero the independent sources\nC. Zero only the independent sources, keep the dependent source active, and drive the port with a\ntest source: R = V /I\nTH X X\nD. R is undefined when dependent sources are present; only the Norton form can be used\nTH\n","summary":"Deactivate independent sources, keep dependent sources, and use a test source.","steps":["An independent ideal voltage source set to zero volts becomes a short circuit. An independent ideal current source set to zero amperes becomes an open circuit. This removes independent excitation while preserving the network's response.","A dependent source must remain active because its value changes in response to the circuit's control voltage or current. Removing it changes the network whose resistance is being measured.","Apply a test voltage \\(V_X\\) at the port and calculate the current \\(I_X\\) entering the network, or apply a test current and calculate the voltage. Then \\(R_{TH}=V_X/I_X\\). The Norton resistance is the same, and the open-circuit voltage determines the independent source in the equivalent."],"prerequisites":["Independent versus controlled sources.","Thévenin and Norton equivalents.","Test-source method and passive sign convention."],"pitfall":"A dependent source might evaluate to zero in a particular solution, but that is a consequence of solving the circuit, not permission to remove the source beforehand.","formula":"\\[R_{TH}=\\frac{V_X}{I_X}=R_N,\\qquad I_N=\\frac{V_{TH}}{R_{TH}}\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":20,"page":11,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"Miller's theorem","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":"D","questionImage":"assets/questions/20-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/20-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 20\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · Miller's theorem · Medium\nMiller's theorem replaces a floating impedance Z between two nodes with voltage gain A = v /v by two\nv o i\ngrounded impedances. Which statement is correct?\nA. Z = Z(1 − A ) at the input and Z = Z(1 − 1/A ) at the output, with A the gain measured with Z\n1 v 2 v v\nremoved\nB. Z = Z·A at the input and Z = Z/A at the output\n1 v 2 v\nC. The equivalence also preserves the internal currents of the circuit\nD. Z = Z/(1 − A ) at the input and Z = Z/(1 − 1/A ) at the output, with A the gain measured with Z in\n1 v 2 v v\nplace\nE. It applies even when Z is the only signal path between the two nodes\n","summary":"Miller impedances use the actual voltage ratio across the original impedance.","steps":["Let the original circuit satisfy \\(A_v=v_2/v_1\\). The current drawn from node 1 by the bridging impedance is \\(i_1=(v_1-v_2)/Z=v_1(1-A_v)/Z\\). A grounded impedance drawing the same current must be \\(Z_1=Z/(1-A_v)\\).","At node 2, \\(i_2=(v_2-v_1)/Z=v_2(1-1/A_v)/Z\\), so \\(Z_2=Z/(1-1/A_v)\\). These equivalents reproduce the loading at the two nodes for that voltage relationship.","The gain used must be the ratio with the original impedance in place. Using a gain computed after simply removing the impedance is an extra approximation and is valid only when that removal does not materially affect the relevant gain."],"prerequisites":["Ohm's law for complex impedance.","Voltage gain as a signed ratio.","Equivalent node currents versus physical branch currents."],"pitfall":"For an inverting gain, \\(A_v<0\\), so \\(1-A_v=1+|A_v|\\). Do not multiply the impedance by this factor; the impedance is divided, while a capacitance is multiplied.","formula":"\\[Z_1=\\frac{Z}{1-A_v},\\qquad Z_2=\\frac{Z}{1-1/A_v}\\]","sourceNote":"The bank's closing sentence is too broad: the theorem itself does not fail merely because removing Z changes the gain. The error is using the gain of that altered circuit instead of the original loaded ratio. If Z is the only signal path, a naive removal can destroy the relation needed for a useful Miller replacement. <a href=\"https://wiki.analog.com/university/courses/electronics/text/chapter-9#millers_theorem\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Analog Devices: Miller's theorem</a>."},{"id":21,"page":12,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"Miller's theorem","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":"E","questionImage":"assets/questions/21-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/21-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 21\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · Miller's theorem · Medium\nA common-source stage has inverting gain |A| = 10, C = 20 fF and C = 50 fF. By Miller's theorem the\nGD GS\ntotal input capacitance is:\nA. 70 fF\nB. 90 fF\nC. 220 fF\nD. 250 fF\nE. 270 fF\n","summary":"Miller multiplication raises the total input capacitance to 270 fF.","steps":["The common-source stage is inverting, so the signed voltage gain is \\(A_v=-10\\), even though the question supplies its magnitude as 10.","The gate-drain capacitor bridges input and output. Its equivalent input capacitance is \\(C_{M,in}=C_{GD}(1-A_v)=20\\,\\mathrm{fF}\\times11=220\\,\\mathrm{fF}\\). The moving drain increases the voltage change across that capacitor.","The gate-source capacitor is already connected to the AC-grounded source, so it adds directly: \\(C_{in}=C_{GS}+C_{M,in}=50+220=270\\,\\mathrm{fF}\\). For comparison, the corresponding output Miller contribution is only \\(20(1+1/10)=22\\,\\mathrm{fF}\\)."],"prerequisites":["Miller theorem for a capacitor.","Signed gain of a common-source stage.","Parallel capacitance addition."],"pitfall":"220 fF is only the Miller contribution. The total also includes the 50 fF gate-source capacitance. Using \\(1-|A|\\) instead of \\(1-A_v\\) loses the inversion sign.","formula":"\\[C_{in}=C_{GS}+(1+|A_v|)C_{GD}=270\\,\\mathrm{fF}\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":22,"page":12,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"Miller's theorem","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"A","questionImage":"assets/questions/22-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/22-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 22\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · Miller's theorem · Medium\nA common-source stage is driven from R = 2 kΩ and has inverting gain |A| = 15, C = 40 fF and C = 10\nsig GS GD\nfF. The input pole frequency f = 1/(2πR C ) is approximately:\np sig in\nA. 400 MHz\nB. 800 MHz\nC. 1.6 GHz\nD. 4 GHz\n","summary":"The Miller-multiplied input capacitance is 200 fF, giving an input pole near 400 MHz.","steps":["The signed voltage gain is \\(A_v=-15\\). The gate-to-drain capacitor connects two nodes that move in opposite directions, so the voltage across it changes by 16 times the input change. Its input equivalent is \\(C_{GD}(1-A_v)=10(1+15)=160\\,\\mathrm{fF}\\).","Add the capacitance already connected from gate to AC ground: \\(C_{in}=C_{GS}+C_{GD}(1-A_v)=40+160=200\\,\\mathrm{fF}\\).","The source resistance and this input capacitance form the stated first-order pole: \\[f_p=\\frac{1}{2\\pi(2\\times10^3)(200\\times10^{-15})}=3.98\\times10^8\\,\\mathrm{Hz}.\\] Thus the nearest choice is 400 MHz."],"prerequisites":["Miller's theorem for a capacitor between input and output","Signed gain of a common-source amplifier","RC pole frequency and unit conversion"],"pitfall":"Adding only 40 fF and 10 fF predicts about 1.6 GHz. That ignores the extra charge needed when the drain moves opposite to the gate.","formula":"\\[C_{in}=C_{GS}+(1-A_v)C_{GD},\\qquad f_p=\\frac{1}{2\\pi R_{sig}C_{in}}.\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":23,"page":13,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"I–V curves","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"C","questionImage":"assets/questions/23-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/23-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 23\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · I–V curves · Medium\nThe figure shows one output curve of a MOSFET at fixed V . In saturation it passes through (0.5 V, 1.00\nGS\nmA) and (1.5 V, 1.10 mA). The small-signal output resistance r and the Early voltage V are approximately:\no A\nA. r ≈ 1 kΩ, V ≈ 1 V\no A\nB. r ≈ 100 kΩ, V ≈ 95 V\no A\nC. r ≈ 10 kΩ, V ≈ 9.5 V\no A\nD. r ≈ 10 kΩ, V ≈ 0.1 V\no A\n","summary":"The saturation slope gives 10 kΩ; extrapolating that line to zero current gives an Early-voltage magnitude of 9.5 V.","steps":["At fixed gate voltage, the output conductance is the slope of the saturation curve: \\(g_{ds}=\\Delta I_D/\\Delta V_{DS}=0.10\\,\\mathrm{mA}/1.0\\,\\mathrm{V}=0.10\\,\\mathrm{mS}\\). Therefore \\(r_o=1/g_{ds}=10\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}\\).","Write the straight saturation line as \\(I_D=(V_{DS}+V_A)/r_o\\). At the first marked point, \\(V_A=I_Dr_o-V_{DS}=(1.00\\,\\mathrm{mA})(10\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega})-0.5\\,\\mathrm{V}=9.5\\,\\mathrm{V}\\).","The actual horizontal-axis intercept is negative: \\(V_{DS}=-V_A=-9.5\\,\\mathrm{V}\\). Its positive magnitude is the parameter requested. The associated channel-length-modulation coefficient is \\(\\lambda=1/V_A\\approx0.105\\,\\mathrm{V}^{-1}\\)."],"prerequisites":["Output resistance as the inverse local I–V slope","Linear extrapolation","Early voltage and channel-length modulation"],"pitfall":"Do not confuse a conductance of 0.1 mS with a resistance of 0.1 kΩ, or the parameter λ with the Early voltage. Also, the exact extrapolation subtracts the operating VDS from IDro.","formula":"\\[r_o=\\frac{\\Delta V_{DS}}{\\Delta I_D},\\qquad V_A=I_Dr_o-V_{DS}.\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":24,"page":14,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"Source follower (PMOS)","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"B","questionImage":"assets/questions/24-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/24-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 24\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · Source follower (PMOS) · Medium\nThe source follower is rebuilt with a PMOS transistor whose bulk is tied to its source (figure), with R\nS\nreturned to V and the drain at ground. Compared with the NMOS follower, which statement is correct?\nDD\nA. The output is level-shifted downward, V = V − |V |, exactly as in the NMOS follower, since only\nout in GS\nthe carrier type changes\nB. The output is level-shifted upward, V = V + |V |, and because the PMOS sits in its own n-well\nout in GS\nthe body can be tied to the source, so V = 0 and there is no body effect\nSB\nC. The body effect is stronger, because the PMOS source now moves with respect to the n-well\npotential\nD. The gain exceeds 1, because the lower hole mobility raises 1/g\nm\n","summary":"A PMOS follower places its source above its gate, and tying its own well to its source removes source-to-body bias.","steps":["For an on PMOS, \\(V_{SG}=V_S-V_G=|V_{GS}|>0\\). The output is the source, so \\(V_{out}=V_{in}+|V_{GS}|\\): the DC level shift is upward.","With the n-well connected directly to the source, \\(V_{SB}=0\\) as the source moves. The threshold therefore does not acquire an additional signal-dependent shift from body effect.","The follower still has local negative feedback: an output rise reduces the change in gate-to-source voltage that drives the current. Neglecting output resistance, its small-signal gain is \\(g_mR_S/(1+g_mR_S)\\), positive and less than one. Lower hole mobility does not reverse that feedback or make the gain exceed one."],"prerequisites":["PMOS voltage polarities","Common-drain voltage follower","Body effect and the role of an isolated n-well"],"pitfall":"NMOS and PMOS followers have the same noninverting small-signal character but opposite DC level shifts. A body tied to its moving source does not produce a changing body bias.","formula":"\\[V_{out}=V_{in}+V_{SG},\\qquad V_{SB}=0,\\qquad A_v=\\frac{g_mR_S}{1+g_mR_S}<1.\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":25,"page":15,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"Common source (PMOS)","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"D","questionImage":"assets/questions/25-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/25-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 25\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · Common source (PMOS) · Medium\nThe common-source stage is rebuilt with a PMOS transistor of the same W/L (figure): the source sits at V ,\nDD\nR returns the drain to ground, and the device is biased at the same |V | as the NMOS version. With μ ≈\nD OV p\n0.5·μ , compared with the NMOS stage:\nn\nA. |I | drops to half but g is unchanged, since g depends only on V\nD m m OV\nB. g drops to half but |I | is unchanged, so the gain halves at the same power\nm D\nC. Nothing changes except the sign of the gain, which becomes positive because the PMOS pulls the\noutput up\nD. |I | and g both drop to about half, so |A | = g R halves; restoring g takes roughly twice the\nD m v m D m\nwidth, which doubles C\nGS\n","summary":"At the same geometry and overdrive, halving mobility halves both current and transconductance; doubling width restores transconductance at a capacitance cost.","steps":["In the long-channel model, \\(|I_D|=\\tfrac12\\mu C_{ox}(W/L)|V_{OV}|^2\\) and \\(g_m=\\mu C_{ox}(W/L)|V_{OV}|\\). Both are directly proportional to mobility when geometry and overdrive are fixed.","Since \\(\\mu_p\\approx0.5\\mu_n\\), both \\(|I_D|\\) and \\(g_m\\) become approximately half their NMOS values. With the same resistive load and the assumptions of the question, \\(|A_v|=g_mR_D\\) halves too.","Increasing PMOS width by a factor of two compensates the factor-of-two mobility loss. However, the channel capacitance \\(C_{GS}\\approx\\tfrac23WLC_{ox}\\) also doubles relative to the original width, reducing speed for a given restored transconductance.","The PMOS common-source stage is still inverting: raising the gate toward the source decreases \\(V_{SG}\\), reduces current through the resistor to ground, and lowers the drain voltage."],"prerequisites":["Square-law saturation current","Transconductance as a gate-voltage derivative","Common-source gain sign","Gate capacitance scaling with width"],"pitfall":"Changing carrier type does not change the common-source stage into a noninverting amplifier. At fixed overdrive, mobility affects both ID and gm, not just one of them.","formula":"\\[|I_D|\\propto\\mu W,\\qquad g_m\\propto\\mu W,\\qquad C_{GS}\\propto W\\quad(L,|V_{OV}|\\text{ fixed}).\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":26,"page":16,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"Small-signal parameters","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"C","questionImage":"assets/questions/26-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/26-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 26\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · Small-signal parameters · Medium\nHow does the transconductance of a MOSFET in saturation (square law) change with the overdrive V =\nOV\nV − V (figure)?\nGS TH\nA. g rises as V ², in proportion to I\nm OV D\nB. g is independent of V , because it is set by μC W/L alone\nm OV ox\nC. At fixed W/L, g = μC (W/L)V rises linearly with V (while I rises as V ²); at fixed I , g = 2I /V\nm ox OV OV D OV D m D OV\nfalls as 1/V\nOV\nD. At fixed W/L, g falls as 1/V ; at fixed I it rises linearly with V\nm OV D OV\n","summary":"Transconductance rises with overdrive at fixed geometry, but falls with overdrive when current is held fixed by resizing the device.","steps":["Define \\(K=\\mu C_{ox}W/L\\). Differentiating \\(I_D=\\tfrac12KV_{OV}^2\\) with respect to \\(V_{GS}\\) gives \\(g_m=KV_{OV}\\). If geometry is fixed, doubling overdrive doubles transconductance and quadruples current.","Eliminating \\(K\\) gives \\(g_m=2I_D/V_{OV}\\). If current is fixed instead, a larger overdrive requires a smaller \\(W/L\\), and the resulting transconductance decreases inversely with overdrive.","These are two views of the same equation with different quantities held constant. The efficiency \\(g_m/I_D=2/V_{OV}\\) becomes larger at lower overdrive within the validity of the strong-inversion square-law model."],"prerequisites":["Definition of overdrive","Differentiating the square-law current","Distinguishing fixed-current from fixed-geometry comparisons"],"pitfall":"Before applying a scaling rule, identify what is held fixed. You cannot vary overdrive, keep both current and geometry unchanged, and still satisfy the square law.","formula":"\\[g_m=\\mu C_{ox}\\frac WL V_{OV}=\\frac{2I_D}{V_{OV}},\\qquad \\frac{g_m}{I_D}=\\frac{2}{V_{OV}}.\\]","sourceNote":"Do not extrapolate 2/VOV to zero overdrive: the square-law approximation breaks down. In weak inversion, gm/ID approaches 1/(nUT), a finite limit."},{"id":27,"page":17,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"MOS structure (PMOS)","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D","E","F"],"answer":"E","questionImage":"assets/questions/27-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/27-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 27\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · MOS structure (PMOS) · Medium\nThe six diagrams show candidate cross-sections, each with the source, drain and bulk voltages marked and\nthe gate tied to the source (V = 0). The mobile carriers are drawn as circled signs (+ holes, − electrons) and\nGS\nevery pn junction as a diode. Which diagram represents a PMOS transistor with the correct charge-carrier\nstate for the marked voltages?\nA. Diagram A\nB. Diagram B\nC. Diagram C\nD. Diagram D\nE. Diagram E\nF. Diagram F\n","summary":"The correct off-state enhancement PMOS has p+ terminals in an n-well, electrons as well majority carriers, and no hole inversion channel at zero gate-to-source voltage.","steps":["A PMOS uses heavily doped p-type source and drain regions inside an n-type body. The n-well's mobile majority carriers are electrons; holes are its minority carriers until the gate induces inversion.","The well is at the highest marked potential. The source-to-well pn junction is unbiased when source and well share a voltage, while a lower-potential p+ drain makes the drain-to-well junction reverse-biased.","The gate is tied to the source, so \\(V_{GS}=0\\). An enhancement PMOS needs \\(V_{GS}<V_{TH}<0\\), or equivalently \\(V_{SG}>|V_{TH}|\\), to create a hole channel. Diagram E combines the correct structure, majority carriers, diode biases and absent channel.","Diagrams A and B lack the required opposite-type junctions; C depicts an NMOS. D incorrectly assigns holes as n-well majority carriers. F incorrectly draws an inversion channel while the enhancement PMOS has \\(V_{GS}=0\\)."],"prerequisites":["n-type and p-type majority carriers","PMOS cross-section","pn-junction bias polarity","Enhancement-mode turn-on condition"],"pitfall":"Having mobile electrons in the well does not create a conducting hole channel between the p+ terminals. Structure, diode bias and gate-induced inversion must all be checked separately.","formula":"\\[\\text{PMOS on: }V_{SG}>|V_{TH}|.\\qquad V_{GS}=0>V_{TH}\\Rightarrow\\text{off in the ideal model}.\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":28,"page":18,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"MOS structure (PMOS)","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"A","questionImage":"assets/questions/28-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/28-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 28\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · MOS structure (PMOS) · Medium\nThe PMOS in the figure (p+ source and drain in an n-well, V = −0.4 V) has its gate and bulk tied to the\nTH\nsource, so V = V = 0, while the drain is held 0.8 V below the source (V = 0.8 V). The well's majority\nGS BS SD\ncarriers are drawn; the channel region is left open. Is the transistor conducting?\nA. No: at V = 0 > V there is no inversion layer, the source–well junction is unbiased and the drain–\nGS TH\nwell junction is reverse-biased by 0.8 V, so only junction leakage and a subthreshold current several\ndecades below the on-current flow\nB. Yes: V = 0.8 V exceeds |V |, so the drain–source voltage forms the channel and the device\nSD TH\nconducts in saturation\nC. Yes: the 0.8 V drop forward-biases the source–well junction and holes cross the well to the drain\nD. Yes, weakly: V = 0 lies only 0.4 V above V , so a subthreshold current comparable to the on-\nGS TH\ncurrent flows\n","summary":"With gate, body and source tied together, the enhancement PMOS is off; the drain voltage leaves only leakage rather than creating the required hole channel.","steps":["Evaluate the gate condition first: \\(V_{GS}=0\\) while \\(V_{TH}=-0.4\\,\\mathrm V\\). Since \\(V_{GS}\\) is not more negative than threshold, there is no strong-inversion hole channel.","The source and n-well are at the same voltage, so their junction is unbiased. The p+ drain is 0.8 V below the n-well, so the drain-to-well junction is reverse-biased; a large drain-to-source voltage does not imply a forward-biased body diode.","The off-state current is not mathematically zero in a real device. For an illustrative subthreshold swing of \\(80\\,\\mathrm{mV/dec}\\), moving 0.4 V away from threshold suppresses subthreshold current by about \\(0.4/0.080=5\\) decades, or a factor \\(10^5\\) relative to its threshold-region value."],"prerequisites":["PMOS threshold-voltage sign convention","Body diode polarity","Subthreshold swing as volts per current decade"],"pitfall":"VSD exceeding |VTH| is not a turn-on test. Gate-to-source voltage controls inversion; drain-to-source voltage selects the region only after the device has turned on.","formula":"\\[V_{SG}=0<|V_{TH}|=0.4\\,\\mathrm V,\\qquad N_{dec}=\\frac{0.4}{0.080}=5.\\]","sourceNote":"The five-decade estimate compares two weak-inversion bias points using an assumed swing; it is an illustration of leakage suppression, not a precise ratio to every possible strong-inversion on-current."},{"id":29,"page":18,"lecture":2,"lectureName":"SPICE Modeling","topic":"Transient analysis","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"D","questionImage":"assets/questions/29-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/29-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 29\nL2 SPICE Modeling · Transient analysis · Medium\nWhich statement about SPICE transient analysis is correct?\nA. It starts from zero initial conditions on every node and integrates forward with a fixed time step,\nwithout any Newton–Raphson iteration\nB. It linearizes the transistors once at the operating point and keeps those models fixed for the whole\nrun\nC. It computes each time point independently of the previous one, so the time step has no effect on\naccuracy\nD. It starts from the DC operating point, replaces C and L at each time step by integration-derived\nequivalents, and solves each step with Newton–Raphson seeded by the previous point\n","summary":"Transient analysis advances a nonlinear circuit in time, using reactive-element history and iterative solutions at each time point.","steps":["In a normal transient run, the DC operating point supplies a consistent starting condition. Capacitor voltages and inductor currents are state variables whose previous values matter when time advances.","A numerical integration formula turns capacitor and inductor differential equations into equivalent algebraic relationships for the new time point. For example, backward Euler represents a capacitor current as \\(i_C^{(k)}=C[v_C^{(k)}-v_C^{(k-1)}]/\\Delta t\\).","The new circuit equations remain nonlinear because transistor and diode currents depend nonlinearly on voltages. Newton–Raphson iteration updates their linearizations until the new point converges, with the previous solution or a prediction from it serving as an initial guess.","Consequently, this is not a single fixed small-signal model and time points are not independent. The integration method and time-step selection affect both accuracy and convergence."],"prerequisites":["Capacitor and inductor differential equations","DC operating point","Numerical integration","Newton–Raphson solution of nonlinear equations"],"pitfall":"A model linearized once at the DC bias describes AC small-signal analysis, not general transient analysis with large voltage changes.","formula":"\\[i_C=C\\frac{dv_C}{dt}\\quad\\longrightarrow\\quad i_C^{(k)}\\approx\\frac C{\\Delta t}v_C^{(k)}-\\frac C{\\Delta t}v_C^{(k-1)}.\\]","sourceNote":"Starting from a computed DC operating point describes the default workflow. Explicit initial conditions and simulator options can override or skip that initialization."},{"id":30,"page":19,"lecture":2,"lectureName":"SPICE Modeling","topic":"Netlist","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":"B","questionImage":"assets/questions/30-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/30-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 30\nL2 SPICE Modeling · Netlist · Medium\nThe circuit in the figure (nodes vdd, in, out and ground 0; M1 is a 1.2 V core NMOS with W = 10 μm, L = 0.5\nμm) is to be simulated in Spectre, whose MOS pin order is drain, gate, source, bulk. Which netlist\nrepresents the circuit correctly?\nA. vdd (vdd 0) vsource dc=1.8\nvin (in 0) vsource dc=0.8\nrd (vdd out) resistor r=10k\nm1 (in out 0 0) nmos1v w=10u l=0.5u\nB. vdd (vdd 0) vsource dc=1.8\nvin (in 0) vsource dc=0.8\nrd (vdd out) resistor r=10k\nm1 (out in 0 0) nmos1v w=10u l=0.5u\nC. vdd (vdd 0) vsource dc=1.8\nvin (in 0) vsource dc=0.8\nrd (out 0) resistor r=10k\nm1 (out in 0 0) nmos1v w=10u l=0.5u\nD. vdd (vdd 0) vsource dc=1.8\nvin (in 0) vsource dc=0.8\nrd (vdd out) resistor r=10k\nm1 (out in 0 vdd) nmos1v w=10u l=0.5u\nE. vdd (vdd 0) vsource dc=1.8\nvin (0 in) vsource dc=0.8\nrd (vdd out) resistor r=10k\nm1 (out in 0 0) nmos1v w=10u l=0.5u\n","summary":"Only netlist B connects the MOS terminals in drain–gate–source–bulk order while preserving the resistor and voltage-source polarities.","steps":["Map each MOS terminal to the schematic before reading the candidate lines: drain is <code>out</code>, gate is <code>in</code>, source is ground <code>0</code>, and bulk is also <code>0</code>. The required instance is <code>m1 (out in 0 0) nmos1v w=10u l=0.5u</code>.","The load connects from the positive supply to the drain, so its nodes must be <code>(vdd out)</code>. The input source <code>vin (in 0) vsource dc=0.8</code> establishes \\(V_{in}-0=+0.8\\,\\mathrm V\\).","A swaps drain and gate. C moves the resistor to ground. D ties the p-type NMOS body to the positive supply, forward-biasing the body-to-source junction. E reverses the input source and therefore sets the gate to \\(-0.8\\,\\mathrm V\\). B is the only topologically correct choice."],"prerequisites":["The stated Spectre MOS pin order","Ground node 0","Voltage-source terminal polarity","NMOS body-junction bias"],"pitfall":"Do not infer MOS pin order from the visual placement of the schematic pins. Also, reversing a two-terminal voltage source changes the sign of its node voltage.","formula":"\\[(D,G,S,B)=(out,in,0,0),\\qquad V_{in}-V_0=+0.8\\,\\mathrm V.\\]","sourceNote":"This answer checks the supplied circuit's connectivity. Using a nominal 1.2 V core model with a 1.8 V supply is not by itself a guarantee that all physical device voltage limits are respected."},{"id":31,"page":20,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Intrinsic carriers","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"D","questionImage":"assets/questions/31-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/31-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 31\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Intrinsic carriers · Medium\nThe intrinsic carrier concentration of silicon is n ≈ 1 × 1010 cm−3 at 300 K. Doubling the absolute\ni\ntemperature to 600 K increases n by roughly:\ni\nA. 2×\nB. 8×\nC. 10³×\nD. 10⁵×\n","summary":"The exponential thermal-generation term makes intrinsic carrier concentration increase by roughly five orders of magnitude.","steps":["Intrinsic concentration follows approximately \\(n_i(T)=A T^{3/2}\\exp[-E_g/(2kT)]\\). Doubling temperature changes both the prefactor and, much more strongly, the exponential.","Taking a ratio eliminates the material constant: \\[\\frac{n_i(600)}{n_i(300)}=2^{3/2}\\exp\\!\\left[\\frac{E_g}{2k}\\left(\\frac1{300}-\\frac1{600}\\right)\\right].\\] With \\(E_g\\approx1.12\\,\\mathrm{eV}\\) and \\(k\\approx8.62\\times10^{-5}\\,\\mathrm{eV/K}\\), the exponent is about 10.8.","Thus the ratio is approximately \\(2.83e^{10.8}\\approx1.4\\times10^5\\). Starting near \\(10^{10}\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\\), this puts the concentration around \\(10^{15}\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\\) to the accuracy requested."],"prerequisites":["Intrinsic electron–hole generation","Boltzmann exponential","Absolute temperature in kelvin","Ratios and orders of magnitude"],"pitfall":"Doubling temperature does not merely double concentration. Ignoring the exponential misses almost the entire effect.","formula":"\\[n_i\\propto T^{3/2}e^{-E_g/(2kT)},\\qquad \\frac{n_i(600)}{n_i(300)}\\sim10^5.\\]","sourceNote":"This is an order-of-magnitude estimate using an approximately constant band gap. Silicon's band gap also changes with temperature, so the ratio is not an exact material constant."},{"id":32,"page":20,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"pn junction","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"C","questionImage":"assets/questions/32-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/32-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 32\nL3 Transistor Impairments · pn junction · Medium\nIn a pn junction at equilibrium, which statement is correct?\nA. Mobile electrons and holes inside the depletion region carry a steady built-in current around the\nloop\nB. The built-in field drives a net diffusion current from the n side to the p side\nC. Fixed ionized dopants form a space charge whose field balances carrier diffusion; the Fermi level is\nflat across the junction\nD. Forward bias widens the depletion region and reverse bias narrows it\n","summary":"At equilibrium, fixed depletion-region ions create an electric field that balances diffusion, with a constant Fermi level and zero net current.","steps":["Electrons initially diffuse from the n side toward the p side and holes diffuse in the opposite direction because of concentration gradients. Recombination near the junction exposes fixed positive donor ions on the n side and fixed negative acceptor ions on the p side.","These ions form the depletion-region space charge. Its electric field drives drift currents that oppose diffusion. At thermal equilibrium, the drift and diffusion contributions cancel for each carrier species, so there is no net terminal current.","A uniform equilibrium Fermi level expresses the absence of a driving force for net carrier transfer. An applied reverse bias increases the barrier and widens the depletion region; forward bias lowers the barrier and narrows it."],"prerequisites":["Diffusion from carrier concentration gradients","Drift in an electric field","Ionized donors and acceptors","Equilibrium Fermi level"],"pitfall":"The built-in electric field drives drift, not diffusion. A junction can have internal fields and opposing microscopic current components while its net equilibrium current remains zero.","formula":"\\[J_n^{drift}+J_n^{diff}=0,\\qquad J_p^{drift}+J_p^{diff}=0\\quad(\\text{equilibrium}).\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":33,"page":21,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"I–V derivation","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"B","questionImage":"assets/questions/33-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/33-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 33\nL3 Transistor Impairments · I–V derivation · Medium\nThe first-order derivation of the MOS I–V characteristics (figure) rests on three assumptions. Which set\nstates them correctly?\nA. (1) The current is carried by the depletion charge; (2) the lateral field sets the channel charge; (3)\nthe carrier velocity is constant at v along the channel\nsat\nB. (1) The current is carried by the mobile inversion charge; (2) the vertical field sets that charge\nthrough the local gate-to-channel voltage, the gradual-channel approximation; (3) the carrier\nvelocity is proportional to the lateral field, v = μE\nC. (1) The current is driven by diffusion; (2) the channel charge is set by the substrate doping N ; (3)\nsub\nthe carrier velocity is proportional to the vertical field\nD. (1) The current is carried by the inversion charge; (2) the channel charge is uniform along the\nchannel; (3) the carrier velocity saturates at the source end\n","summary":"The long-channel derivation combines mobile inversion charge, gate-controlled local charge, and drift velocity proportional to the lateral electric field.","steps":["The conducting sheet consists of mobile inversion electrons. Using \\(Q_n\\) as the positive magnitude of their charge per area, the current magnitude is \\(I_D=WQ_n(y)v(y)\\). Fixed depletion ions do not transport the drain current.","The gradual-channel approximation separates vertical electrostatics from the slowly varying channel potential. The local inversion-charge magnitude is \\(Q_n(y)=C_{ox}[V_{GS}-V_{TH}-V(y)]\\), so it is generally not uniform along the channel.","For a sufficiently low lateral field, carrier speed is proportional to field: \\(v=\\mu E_y\\). Combining this with the local charge and integrating from source to drain gives \\[I_DL=W\\mu C_{ox}\\int_0^{V_{DS}}(V_{OV}-V)\\,dV,\\] hence the familiar triode equation.","Velocity saturation violates the linear velocity–field assumption at high fields, while very short channels also weaken the separation of vertical and lateral electrostatics. Those effects require refinements to the square-law model."],"prerequisites":["Mobile inversion versus fixed depletion charge","Gradual-channel approximation","Drift velocity and mobility","Integration of local channel current"],"pitfall":"The vertical field controls how much charge is present; the lateral field drives that charge along the channel. Mixing up their roles leads to the wrong assumptions.","formula":"\\[I_D=\\mu C_{ox}\\frac WL\\left(V_{OV}V_{DS}-\\frac{V_{DS}^2}{2}\\right).\\]","sourceNote":"The question denotes the inversion-charge magnitude by Qn. The signed electron sheet charge is negative: Qinv = −Cox[VOV − V(y)]."},{"id":34,"page":22,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"I–V derivation","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"A","questionImage":"assets/questions/34-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/34-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 34\nL3 Transistor Impairments · I–V derivation · Medium\nAlong the channel of an NMOS in triode (figure) the channel potential V(y) rises from 0 at the source to V\nDS\nat the drain. Which statement about the inversion charge per unit area Q (y) is correct?\nn\nA. Q (y) = C [V − V(y) − V] is largest at the source and decreases toward the drain, where the gate-\nn ox GS t\nto-channel voltage is smallest; it reaches zero at the drain end when V = V − V (pinch-off)\nDS GS t\nB. Q (y) = C [V − V] everywhere, because the gate voltage is the same along the whole channel\nn ox GS t\nC. Q (y) is smallest at the source and grows toward the drain, where the lateral field is strongest\nn\nD. Q (y) = C ·V(y): the charge is proportional to the local channel potential, so it is zero at the source\nn ox\n","summary":"Channel potential rises toward the drain, reducing the local gate overdrive and therefore reducing the magnitude of inversion charge.","steps":["The relevant voltage is gate-to-local-channel voltage, \\(V_{GC}(y)=V_{GS}-V(y)\\), not merely the gate voltage relative to the source. The charge magnitude is \\(Q_n(y)=C_{ox}[V_{OV}-V(y)]\\).","At the source, \\(V(0)=0\\), so \\(Q_n(0)=C_{ox}V_{OV}\\). At the drain, \\(V(L)=V_{DS}\\), so \\(Q_n(L)=C_{ox}(V_{OV}-V_{DS})\\). The drain-end charge is smaller whenever \\(V_{DS}>0\\).","Because steady-state current is the same at every channel position, lower charge density toward the drain requires higher carrier velocity in the gradual-channel model. At \\(V_{DS}=V_{OV}\\), the ideal drain-end inversion charge goes to zero: this is the onset of pinch-off and long-channel saturation."],"prerequisites":["Local channel potential","Gate-induced inversion charge","Current continuity","Pinch-off condition"],"pitfall":"The gate's potential is uniform, but its voltage relative to the channel is not. A larger lateral field near the drain does not imply more inversion charge there.","formula":"\\[Q_n(0)=C_{ox}V_{OV},\\qquad Q_n(L)=C_{ox}(V_{OV}-V_{DS}),\\qquad V_{DS,sat}=V_{OV}.\\]","sourceNote":"Qn is a positive charge magnitude in the question. The physical electron charge is negative. Current becomes independent of VDS only in the ideal model without channel-length modulation."},{"id":35,"page":22,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Threshold voltage","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"C","questionImage":"assets/questions/35-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/35-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 35\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Threshold voltage · Medium\nIn V = Φ + 2Φ + Q /C − Q /C , the positive fixed oxide/interface charge Q :\nT MS F dep ox ox ox ox\nA. Raises V by Q /C , since the extra charge must be imaged by the gate\nT ox ox\nB. Has no effect, because the gate screens charge located at the interface\nC. Lowers V by Q /C , and its drift over time is one mechanism of transistor aging\nT ox ox\nD. Affects only PMOS devices, where the substrate is n-type\n","summary":"Positive fixed oxide charge helps attract electrons, so less positive gate voltage is required to form an NMOS channel.","steps":["An NMOS reaches threshold when the gate-induced electrostatics produce sufficient electron inversion at a p-type surface. Positive charge near the oxide–silicon interface already helps attract those negative carriers.","The threshold expression includes this assistance as a negative term, \\(-Q_{ox}/C_{ox}\\). Increasing positive oxide charge by \\(\\Delta Q_{ox}\\) therefore changes the threshold by \\(\\Delta V_{TH}=-\\Delta Q_{ox}/C_{ox}\\).","Since the oxide capacitance per area is \\(C_{ox}=\\varepsilon_{ox}/t_{ox}\\), the same added charge produces a larger voltage shift when the oxide is thicker. Changes in trapped charge over device life can therefore alter threshold."],"prerequisites":["NMOS inversion electrostatics","Threshold-voltage terms","Capacitance per area and oxide thickness","Charge-induced voltage shifts"],"pitfall":"Positive oxide charge does not require a more positive gate to invert the surface; it already supplies part of the electrostatic effect that the gate otherwise must provide.","formula":"\\[\\Delta V_{TH}=-\\frac{\\Delta Q_{ox}}{C_{ox}}=-\\frac{\\Delta Q_{ox}t_{ox}}{\\varepsilon_{ox}}.\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":36,"page":22,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Threshold voltage","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"B","questionImage":"assets/questions/36-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/36-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 36\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Threshold voltage · Medium\nWhich change raises the magnitude of the threshold voltage |V | of an NMOS transistor?\nT\nA. Lighter substrate doping and thinner gate oxide\nB. Heavier substrate doping and thicker gate oxide\nC. Higher operating temperature\nD. More positive fixed oxide charge Q\nox\n","summary":"Heavier p-type substrate doping and a thicker gate oxide increase the gate voltage needed to reach NMOS threshold in the stated model.","steps":["For a p-type body, \\(\\Phi_F=U_T\\ln(N_A/n_i)\\) increases with acceptor concentration. Threshold requires surface band bending of approximately \\(2\\Phi_F\\), so heavier doping increases that contribution.","The magnitude of depletion charge at threshold is \\(Q_{dep}=\\sqrt{4q\\varepsilon_{Si}N_A\\Phi_F}\\). More doping increases the charge that must be supported through the gate oxide.","Making the oxide thicker decreases \\(C_{ox}=\\varepsilon_{ox}/t_{ox}\\). Consequently the voltage drop \\(Q_{dep}/C_{ox}\\) increases. Both changes in choice B push the positive NMOS threshold upward under the assumptions used here.","The other listed trends go the opposite way in the question's model: lighter doping and thinner oxide lower threshold, higher temperature usually lowers it, and positive fixed oxide charge contributes \\(-Q_{ox}/C_{ox}\\)."],"prerequisites":["Threshold as surface potential plus oxide voltage","Doping dependence of Fermi potential and depletion charge","Cox inversely proportional to oxide thickness"],"pitfall":"A thicker oxide is a weaker capacitor: for the same depletion charge it needs more voltage, not less.","formula":"\\[V_{TH}=V_{FB}+2\\Phi_F+\\frac{Q_{dep}}{C_{ox}},\\qquad Q_{dep}=\\sqrt{4q\\varepsilon_{Si}N_A\\Phi_F}.\\]","sourceNote":"This trend assumes the usual enhancement NMOS regime and the stated first-order model. In a full process comparison, work functions, oxide charge and other parameters may change as well."},{"id":37,"page":23,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Body effect","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":"E","questionImage":"assets/questions/37-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/37-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 37\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Body effect · Medium\nThe body transconductance g of a MOSFET in saturation is:\nmb\nA. Equal to g , since the bulk acts as a second gate\nm\nB. g ·γ, independent of V\nm SB\nC. Zero whenever the device is in saturation\nD. g ·(2Φ + V ), growing with reverse body bias\nm F SB\nE. η·g with η = γ / (2√(2Φ + V )), typically a fraction of g\nm F SB m\n","summary":"The body acts as a weaker second gate: its transconductance is the gate transconductance multiplied by the body-effect sensitivity η.","steps":["Start from the body-effect threshold formula \\(V_{TH}=V_{TH0}+\\gamma[\\sqrt{2\\Phi_F+V_{SB}}-\\sqrt{2\\Phi_F}]\\). Its derivative with respect to reverse body bias is \\(\\partial V_{TH}/\\partial V_{SB}=\\gamma/[2\\sqrt{2\\Phi_F+V_{SB}}]=\\eta\\).","Increasing threshold at fixed gate voltage reduces current, so \\(\\partial I_D/\\partial V_{TH}=-g_m\\). Since \\(V_{BS}=-V_{SB}\\), differentiating with respect to \\(V_{BS}\\) introduces another minus sign.","The two signs cancel, yielding the positive body transconductance \\(g_{mb}=\\partial I_D/\\partial V_{BS}=\\eta g_m\\). The dimensionless factor \\(\\eta\\) is normally less than one, so the body controls current less effectively than the gate."],"prerequisites":["Body-effect threshold formula","Chain rule","The distinction between VSB and VBS","Small-signal transconductance"],"pitfall":"γ alone is not the multiplying factor: it has units of square-root volts. Also, saturation does not remove body effect; the saturation current still depends on threshold.","formula":"\\[g_{mb}=\\eta g_m,\\qquad \\eta=\\frac{\\gamma}{2\\sqrt{2\\Phi_F+V_{SB}}}.\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":38,"page":23,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Gate capacitance","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"A","questionImage":"assets/questions/38-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/38-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 38\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Gate capacitance · Medium\nThe intrinsic (channel) gate capacitances of a MOSFET in saturation are:\nA. C = ⅔WLC , C ≈ 0 (overlap WC only), C ≈ 0\nGS ox GD ov GB\nB. C = ½WLC , C = ½WLC , C = 0\nGS ox GD ox GB\nC. C = WLC , C = WLC , C = 0\nGS ox GD ox GB\nD. C = ⅓WLC , C = ⅓WLC , C = ⅓WLC\nGS ox GD ox GB ox\n","summary":"In the ideal long-channel saturation model, the intrinsic gate capacitance is associated with the source; the drain retains only its overlap contribution.","steps":["At saturation, the inversion channel tapers toward zero charge at the drain-end pinch-off point. The distributed charge is therefore not the same as the uniform channel of deep triode.","Integrating the saturation charge distribution gives a channel-charge magnitude \\(Q_{ch}=\\tfrac23WLC_{ox}V_{OV}\\). Its derivative with respect to gate-to-source voltage gives \\(C_{GS,int}=\\tfrac23WLC_{ox}\\).","In this first-order model, the pinched-off drain does not control intrinsic channel charge, so \\(C_{GD,int}\\approx0\\). The inversion channel screens the bulk, giving \\(C_{GB}\\approx0\\). Physical gate overlap still adds \\(WC_{ov}\\) at both source and drain."],"prerequisites":["Pinch-off in saturation","Capacitance as a charge derivative","Intrinsic versus overlap capacitance","Gate shielding by the inversion channel"],"pitfall":"The one-half/one-half split belongs to a nearly uniform deep-triode channel. Zero intrinsic CGD does not mean the real device has no gate-to-drain capacitance.","formula":"\\[C_{GS,int}=\\frac23WLC_{ox},\\qquad C_{GD,int}\\approx0,\\qquad C_{GB}\\approx0.\\]","sourceNote":"These are the bank's quasi-static, long-channel capacitance approximations. Real compact models include bias-dependent charge partition and additional parasitic effects."},{"id":39,"page":24,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"I–V curves","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"D","questionImage":"assets/questions/39-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/39-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 39\nL3 Transistor Impairments · I–V curves · Medium\nThe figure shows I versus V of an NMOS in saturation (square law, λ = 0): I = 45 μA at V = 0.7 V and 180\nD GS D GS\nμA at V = 1.0 V. The threshold voltage and K = μ C (W/L) are:\nGS n ox\nA. V = 0.4 V, K = 0.5 mA/V²\nTH\nB. V = 0.3 V, K = 1 mA/V²\nTH\nC. V = 0.55 V, K = 2 mA/V²\nTH\nD. V = 0.4 V, K = 1 mA/V²\nTH\n","summary":"A fourfold current increase means doubled overdrive; the two bias points imply VTH = 0.4 V and K = 1 mA/V².","steps":["With \\(I_D=\\tfrac12K(V_{GS}-V_{TH})^2\\), take the ratio of the two currents so the unknown \\(K\\) cancels. Since \\(180/45=4\\), the positive overdrives satisfy \\(1.0-V_{TH}=2(0.7-V_{TH})\\).","Solving gives \\(V_{TH}=0.4\\,\\mathrm V\\). The two overdrives are then 0.3 V and 0.6 V, correctly differing by a factor of two.","Use either measurement to find \\(K\\): \\[K=\\frac{2I_D}{V_{OV}^2}=\\frac{2(180\\,\\mu\\mathrm A)}{(0.6\\,\\mathrm V)^2}=1\\,\\mathrm{mA/V^2}.\\] Substitution into the first point gives \\(\\tfrac12(1)(0.3)^2\\,\\mathrm{mA}=45\\,\\mu\\mathrm A\\), checking the result."],"prerequisites":["Square-law saturation current","Taking square roots of current ratios","Threshold as the √ID–VGS intercept","Definition of K in this bank"],"pitfall":"Do not omit the one-half in ID = KVOV²/2. Some textbooks absorb it into a differently defined parameter; this question explicitly does not.","formula":"\\[\\sqrt{\\frac{I_2}{I_1}}=\\frac{V_{GS2}-V_{TH}}{V_{GS1}-V_{TH}},\\qquad K=\\frac{2I_D}{V_{OV}^2}.\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":40,"page":24,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Gate capacitance","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"D","questionImage":"assets/questions/40-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/40-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 40\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Gate capacitance · Medium\nIn the triode region (V ≪ V − V , nearly uniform channel, figure), the intrinsic gate capacitances of a\nDS GS TH\nMOSFET are:\nA. C = ⅔WLC , C ≈ 0, C ≈ 0\nGS ox GD GB\nB. C = C = 0 (overlap only), C = WLC\nGS GD GB ox\nC. C = WLC , C = 0, C = 0\nGS ox GD GB\nD. C = C = ½WLC , C ≈ 0\nGS GD ox GB\n","summary":"A nearly uniform deep-triode channel shares the intrinsic gate capacitance equally between source and drain and screens the bulk.","steps":["The condition \\(V_{DS}\\ll V_{OV}\\) means the local channel potential varies little compared with the overdrive. The inversion sheet is nearly uniform and its total gate-to-channel capacitance is approximately \\(WLC_{ox}\\).","Both ends of that conducting sheet are connected to terminals. Under this symmetric deep-triode approximation, source and drain each receive half of the intrinsic capacitance: \\(C_{GS,int}=C_{GD,int}=\\tfrac12WLC_{ox}\\).","The inversion layer supplies the incremental charge and screens the substrate, so the intrinsic gate-to-bulk capacitance is approximately zero. Add \\(WC_{ov}\\) to each gate-to-source and gate-to-drain value when overlap is included."],"prerequisites":["Deep-triode condition","Uniform inversion channel","Intrinsic versus overlap capacitance","Screening by mobile channel charge"],"pitfall":"Do not use the saturation values of two-thirds and zero merely because the transistor is on. The channel shape and connection to the drain determine the capacitance split.","formula":"\\[C_{GS,int}=C_{GD,int}=\\frac12WLC_{ox},\\qquad C_{GB}\\approx0.\\]","sourceNote":"The equal split is the stated nearly uniform, low-VDS approximation; it is not exact throughout the entire triode region."},{"id":41,"page":25,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Gate capacitance","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"A","questionImage":"assets/questions/41-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/41-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 41\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Gate capacitance · Medium\nWith the transistor off (V < V , no channel, figure), the gate capacitances of a MOSFET are:\nGS TH\nA. C and C reduce to the overlap terms WC , and C is the series combination of the oxide\nGS GD ov GB\ncapacitance WLC and the depletion capacitance C\nox dep\nB. C = C = ½WLC and C = 0, as in triode\nGS GD ox GB\nC. C = C = C = 0, since no charge is stored without a channel\nGS GD GB\nD. C = WLC + C (parallel combination) and C = C = 0\nGB ox dep GS GD\n","summary":"Without an inversion channel, gate coupling to source and drain is mainly overlap, while the depleted body is reached through oxide and depletion capacitances in series.","steps":["Below threshold in the depleted off state, no conducting inversion sheet connects the gate-induced charge to source and drain. Their intrinsic channel capacitance contributions disappear, but the geometric overlaps remain: \\(C_{GS}\\approx C_{GD}\\approx WC_{ov}\\).","An incremental gate voltage is shared between the oxide and the depletion region in the substrate. Those two voltage drops carry the same incremental charge, so their capacitances combine in series rather than in parallel.","Writing \\(C_{ox,total}=WLC_{ox}\\), the gate-to-body capacitance is \\(C_{GB}=C_{ox,total}C_{dep}/(C_{ox,total}+C_{dep})\\), which is smaller than either component. Toward accumulation, the depleted region vanishes and gate-to-body capacitance approaches the full oxide capacitance."],"prerequisites":["Off-state MOS depletion","Series capacitance","Overlap capacitance","Accumulation versus depletion versus inversion"],"pitfall":"No drain current does not imply no stored charge or zero capacitance. Also, a series combination is smaller than its components; adding them directly is incorrect.","formula":"\\[C_{GB}=\\left(\\frac1{WLC_{ox}}+\\frac1{C_{dep}}\\right)^{-1},\\qquad C_{GS}\\approx C_{GD}\\approx WC_{ov}.\\]","sourceNote":"The series-with-depletion description is specifically the depleted off state shown. The broad condition VGS < VTH also includes accumulation, where CGB approaches WLCox."},{"id":42,"page":26,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Threshold voltage","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"B","questionImage":"assets/questions/42-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/42-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 42\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Threshold voltage · Medium\nIn the threshold-voltage expression V = Φ + 2Φ + Q /C (figure), the terms Φ and Φ are:\nTH MS F dep ox MS F\nA. Φ is the surface potential at the onset of inversion; Φ is the built-in potential of the source and\nMS F\ndrain junctions\nB. Φ is the work-function difference between the gate and the silicon substrate, fixed by the gate\nMS\nmaterial; Φ is the bulk Fermi potential (kT/q)·ln(N /n), the separation between the Fermi level of\nF sub i\nthe doped silicon and the intrinsic level\nC. Φ is the voltage dropped across the gate oxide at threshold; Φ is the flat-band voltage of the\nMS F\nMOS capacitor\nD. Φ is the Schottky barrier of the gate contact; Φ is the thermal voltage kT/q\nMS F\n","summary":"ΦMS is the gate–semiconductor work-function difference, and ΦF is the bulk Fermi potential set by doping and temperature.","steps":["A material's work function measures the energy separation from its Fermi level to the vacuum level. Expressed in voltage units, \\(\\Phi_{MS}=\\Phi_M-\\Phi_S\\) is the gate work function minus the semiconductor work function. It contributes to the flat-band voltage.","For the p-type body and the positive-magnitude convention used here, \\(\\Phi_F=U_T\\ln(N_A/n_i)\\). It measures how far doping shifts the bulk Fermi level from the intrinsic level; it is not simply the thermal voltage \\(U_T\\).","At the conventional onset of strong inversion, the surface potential is about \\(2\\Phi_F\\). The gate must supply the work-function offset, this surface band bending, and the oxide voltage needed to support depletion charge, giving the stated threshold expression.","Choice B identifies the two potentials correctly. The other answers confuse them with a junction built-in voltage, the oxide drop, flat-band voltage itself, or a contact barrier."],"prerequisites":["Fermi level and intrinsic level","Work function","Thermal voltage","Flat band and the strong-inversion surface potential"],"pitfall":"ΦF is a doping-dependent potential, not kT/q alone. ΦMS is a difference involving both materials, so it should not generally be treated as a property of the gate alone.","formula":"\\[\\Phi_{MS}=\\Phi_M-\\Phi_S,\\qquad \\Phi_F=\\frac{kT}{q}\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{N_A}{n_i}\\right),\\qquad V_{TH}=\\Phi_{MS}+2\\Phi_F+\\frac{Q_{dep}}{C_{ox}}.\\]","sourceNote":"The official key is B, but the bank's explanation overstates doping independence: the semiconductor work function changes with its Fermi level, so ΦMS generally depends on substrate doping as well as gate material. Also, ΦF need not increase with temperature; ni(T) must be included. See <a href=\"https://www.alan.ece.gatech.edu/ECE3040/Lectures/Lecture23-MOS%20Capacitors.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Georgia Tech, MOS Capacitors</a> for the work-function distinction."},{"id":43,"page":27,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Gate capacitance","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"C","questionImage":"assets/questions/43-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/43-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 43\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Gate capacitance · Medium\nThe figure shows the low-frequency gate capacitance per unit area of a MOS capacitor on a p-type\nsubstrate versus V . Which statement is correct?\nGS\nA. The capacitance is largest in depletion, because the depletion region adds its charge to the oxide\ncharge\nB. In strong inversion the capacitance equals C , because the channel screens the oxide from the\ndep\ngate\nC. In accumulation (V < V ) and in strong inversion (V > V ) the capacitance equals C ; in between\nGS FB GS TH ox\nit falls to the series combination of C and the depletion capacitance, lowest where the depletion\nox\nregion is widest\nD. In accumulation the capacitance is close to zero, because the holes are repelled from the surface\n","summary":"At low frequency, accumulation and strong inversion both approach Cox; depletion lowers the measured capacitance through a series depletion capacitance.","steps":["For a p-type substrate, a sufficiently negative gate relative to flat band attracts holes to the interface. This accumulation layer can respond to the signal, so the dominant dielectric separation is the oxide and the capacitance per area is approximately \\(C_{ox}\\).","Raising the gate above flat band repels holes and produces depletion. The oxide and depleted silicon share the incremental voltage, giving \\(C=(C_{ox}^{-1}+C_{dep}^{-1})^{-1}\\). As depletion width grows, \\(C_{dep}=\\varepsilon_{Si}/W_{dep}\\) decreases and the measured capacitance falls.","Above threshold, a low-frequency signal allows inversion electrons to change in number and supply the incremental surface charge. The depletion width changes little, and the gate capacitance returns toward \\(C_{ox}\\). This gives the high–low–high shape selected by choice C."],"prerequisites":["MOS capacitor accumulation, depletion and inversion","Low-frequency charge response","Series capacitances","Capacitance inversely proportional to depletion width"],"pitfall":"The question explicitly says low frequency. In a high-frequency MOS-capacitor measurement, minority carriers may not follow the signal, so inversion need not return the measured capacitance to Cox.","formula":"\\[C_{acc}\\approx C_{ox},\\qquad C_{depl}=\\frac{C_{ox}C_{dep}}{C_{ox}+C_{dep}},\\qquad C_{inv,LF}\\approx C_{ox}.\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":44,"page":28,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Gate capacitance","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":"C","questionImage":"assets/questions/44-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/44-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 44\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Gate capacitance · Medium\nThe five annotated graphs show candidate curves of C (black), C (blue) and the total gate capacitance\nGS GD\nC = C + C (gray, dashed) versus V for an NMOS at a fixed drain voltage V (source grounded), overlap\nGG GS GD GS D\ncapacitance WC included and C omitted. Each graph carries its capacitance levels and a one-line\nov GB\njustification. Which graph is correct?\nA. Graph A\nB. Graph B\nC. Graph C\nD. Graph D\nE. Graph E\n","summary":"Graph C assigns the intrinsic channel capacitance to the correct terminals in each operating region.","steps":["First locate the two boundaries while the drain voltage stays fixed. Below \\(V_{GS}=V_{TH}\\), the simplified model has no inversion channel. For \\(V_{TH}<V_{GS}<V_D+V_{TH}\\), \\(V_{DS}>V_{GS}-V_{TH}\\), so the device is in saturation. Increasing the gate voltage beyond \\(V_D+V_{TH}\\) puts it in triode.","In saturation, the channel ends before the drain. The intrinsic channel contribution is \\(\\tfrac23 WLC_{ox}\\) at the source and approximately zero at the drain. Adding the geometrical overlaps gives \\(C_{GS}=\\tfrac23 WLC_{ox}+WC_{ov}\\) and \\(C_{GD}=WC_{ov}\\).","In the lecture's triode approximation, the intrinsic channel capacitance divides equally: \\(C_{GS}=C_{GD}=\\tfrac12 WLC_{ox}+WC_{ov}\\). Thus the total rises from \\(2WC_{ov}\\) below threshold, to \\(\\tfrac23 WLC_{ox}+2WC_{ov}\\) in saturation, and then to \\(WLC_{ox}+2WC_{ov}\\) in triode.","Only graph C has the correct total <em>and</em> the correct source/drain allocation. A exchanges the fractions; B adds an incorrect drain channel contribution in saturation; D uses a full channel capacitance at the source; E assigns the saturation contribution to the pinched-off drain."],"prerequisites":["NMOS operating-region inequalities","Intrinsic channel capacitance versus overlap capacitance","Long-channel capacitance partitioning"],"pitfall":"A correct total \\(C_{GG}\\) is insufficient: check which terminal receives the channel contribution.","formula":"\\[C_{GS,\\mathrm{sat}}=\\frac23 WLC_{ox}+WC_{ov},\\qquad C_{GD,\\mathrm{sat}}=WC_{ov}\\]","sourceNote":"The sharp steps and equal split throughout triode are the question bank's simplified capacitance model. Detailed capacitances vary continuously with bias; the equal split is the familiar low-\\(V_{DS}\\) limit."},{"id":45,"page":29,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Gate capacitance","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"B","questionImage":"assets/questions/45-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/45-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 45\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Gate capacitance · Medium\nThe cross-section in the figure marks six capacitances C to C of an NMOS. Which assignment is correct?\n1 6\nA. C : gate–substrate capacitance; C : oxide capacitance; C , C : junction capacitances; C , C : gate\n1 2 3 4 5 6\noverlap capacitances\nB. C : gate–channel oxide capacitance; C : channel–substrate depletion capacitance; C , C : gate\n1 2 3 4\noverlap of the source and drain; C , C : source–bulk and drain–bulk junction capacitances\n5 6\nC. C : gate overlap capacitance; C : gate–channel capacitance; C , C : depletion capacitances; C , C :\n1 2 3 4 5 6\ngate–bulk capacitances\nD. C : gate–channel capacitance; C : gate overlap capacitance; C , C : junction capacitances; C , C :\n1 2 3 4 5 6\nchannel–substrate capacitances\n","summary":"Identify each capacitance by the two physical regions separated by its electric field.","steps":["\\(C_1\\) lies between the gate and the channel across the oxide, so its geometrical scale is \\(WLC_{ox}\\). Its small-signal contribution is distributed between source and drain according to the operating region.","\\(C_2\\) is between the channel/surface region and the substrate across the depleted semiconductor. It is a depletion capacitance, not another oxide overlap.","\\(C_3\\) and \\(C_4\\) arise because the gate extends over the source and drain diffusions. Each contributes approximately \\(WC_{ov}\\), even when the inversion channel is absent.","\\(C_5\\) and \\(C_6\\) belong to the source-body and drain-body p-n junctions. Their depletion widths, and therefore their capacitances, depend on the corresponding reverse-bias voltages. This mapping is exactly option B."],"prerequisites":["MOS cross-section and terminal locations","Oxide, depletion, overlap, and p-n junction capacitances","Bias dependence of junction capacitance"],"pitfall":"Do not identify every capacitance near the gate as an overlap: use the two regions it connects.","formula":"\\[C_{1,\\mathrm{geom}}=WLC_{ox},\\qquad C_3\\approx C_4\\approx WC_{ov},\\qquad C_5=C_{SB},\\quad C_6=C_{DB}\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":46,"page":29,"lecture":4,"lectureName":"Performance Measures of a MOS","topic":"Transit frequency","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"A","questionImage":"assets/questions/46-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/46-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 46\nL4 Performance Measures of a MOS · Transit frequency · Medium\nFor a long-channel MOSFET, ω = g /C ≈ (3/2)·μV /L². Which statement is correct?\nT m gg OV\nA. Raising V raises ω at the cost of current; shrinking L raises ω at the cost of intrinsic gain\nOV T T\nB. ω grows with L², so longer devices are faster\nT\nC. ω is independent of V , since both g and C scale with it\nT OV m gg\nD. ω includes the effect of gate resistance, which is why it limits the power gain\nT\n","summary":"Higher overdrive increases transit frequency, while a shorter channel trades intrinsic gain for speed.","steps":["The short-circuit current gain has magnitude approximately \\(g_m/(\\omega C_{gg})\\). It reaches one at \\(\\omega_T=g_m/C_{gg}\\). This is a current-gain speed metric; \\(f_T=\\omega_T/(2\\pi)\\).","Using \\(g_m=\\mu C_{ox}(W/L)V_{OV}\\) and \\(C_{gg}\\approx\\tfrac23 WLC_{ox}\\), width and oxide capacitance cancel, leaving \\(\\omega_T\\approx\\tfrac32\\mu V_{OV}/L^2\\). At fixed geometry, raising overdrive increases speed but increases \\(I_D\\propto V_{OV}^2\\).","Shortening \\(L\\) raises the ideal long-channel speed strongly, but typically increases channel-length modulation. Since \\(g_mr_o\\approx2/(\\lambda V_{OV})\\), the larger \\(\\lambda\\) lowers intrinsic gain. Gate resistance is central to the separate maximum power-gain frequency \\(f_{max}\\), not included in this ideal expression."],"prerequisites":["Current gain and unity-current-gain frequency","Square-law transconductance and saturation gate capacitance","Channel-length modulation and intrinsic gain"],"pitfall":"Do not confuse \\(\\omega_T\\) in rad/s with \\(f_T\\) in Hz, or current-gain frequency with power-gain frequency.","formula":"\\[\\omega_T=\\frac{g_m}{C_{gg}}\\approx\\frac{3\\mu V_{OV}}{2L^2},\\qquad f_T=\\frac{\\omega_T}{2\\pi}\\]","sourceNote":"These scaling laws neglect short-channel effects and parasitic capacitances; the question explicitly uses the long-channel model."},{"id":47,"page":30,"lecture":4,"lectureName":"Performance Measures of a MOS","topic":"Maximum oscillation frequency","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"B","questionImage":"assets/questions/47-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/47-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 47\nL4 Performance Measures of a MOS · Maximum oscillation frequency · Medium\nThe common-source power gain follows G = (ω /ω)², falling at 20 dB/decade. If a maximum power gain\np max\nof 14 dB (25×) is measured at 20 GHz, f is approximately:\nmax\nA. 50 GHz\nB. 100 GHz\nC. 280 GHz\nD. 500 GHz\n","summary":"A power gain of 25 means the measurement frequency is one fifth of the unity-power-gain frequency.","steps":["Convert power gain from decibels with \\(G_p=10^{G_{p,\\mathrm{dB}}/10}\\). Thus \\(14\\,\\mathrm{dB}\\) is approximately \\(25\\), consistent with the value supplied in the question.","The model states \\(G_p=(f_{max}/f)^2\\), because the factor \\(2\\pi\\) cancels when replacing angular frequencies with ordinary frequencies. Taking the square root gives \\(f_{max}=f\\sqrt{G_p}\\).","Insert \\(f=20\\,\\mathrm{GHz}\\) and \\(G_p=25\\): \\(f_{max}=20\\times5=100\\,\\mathrm{GHz}\\). At this frequency the extrapolated power gain is one, or \\(0\\,\\mathrm{dB}\\)."],"prerequisites":["Power-gain decibels use 10 log10","Square-root scaling","Unity-power-gain definition"],"pitfall":"Multiplying \\(20\\,\\mathrm{GHz}\\) by 25 ignores the square. Multiplying by 14 treats a decibel value as a linear ratio.","formula":"\\[f_{max}=f\\sqrt{10^{G_{p,\\mathrm{dB}}/10}}\\approx20\\,\\mathrm{GHz}\\sqrt{25}=100\\,\\mathrm{GHz}\\]","sourceNote":"The extrapolation assumes the stated inverse-square power-gain law applies at the measurement frequency."},{"id":48,"page":30,"lecture":4,"lectureName":"Performance Measures of a MOS","topic":"gm/ID","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"D","questionImage":"assets/questions/48-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/48-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 48\nL4 Performance Measures of a MOS · gm/ID · Medium\nWhich statement about g /I is correct?\nm D\nA. 1/V in strong inversion; it grows without bound in weak inversion\nOV\nB. Largest in strong inversion, where g is largest\nm\nC. Independent of bias, since g and I scale together\nm D\nD. 2/V in strong inversion; it saturates at 1/(nU ) in weak inversion, its maximum\nOV T\n","summary":"Transconductance efficiency is 2/VOV in square-law strong inversion and approaches a finite weak-inversion limit.","steps":["In strong inversion, write \\(I_D=\\tfrac12 K V_{OV}^2\\). Differentiating with respect to gate voltage gives \\(g_m=K V_{OV}\\), so \\(g_m/I_D=2/V_{OV}\\). A larger overdrive gives less transconductance per unit current.","In weak inversion, \\(I_D\\propto\\exp(V_{GS}/(nU_T))\\). Its derivative is \\(g_m=I_D/(nU_T)\\), so the ratio tends to \\(1/(nU_T)\\), independent of the current level in this approximation.","At room temperature with \\(n\\approx1.3\\text{--}1.5\\), the weak-inversion limit is roughly \\(25\\text{--}30\\,\\mathrm{V}^{-1}\\). Weak inversion maximizes efficiency, although the absolute \\(g_m\\) can be small. The square-law expression must not be extrapolated all the way to zero overdrive."],"prerequisites":["Definition gm = derivative of ID with respect to VGS","Square-law versus exponential current models","Thermal voltage and slope factor"],"pitfall":"High absolute \\(g_m\\) and high \\(g_m/I_D\\) are different goals. The strong-inversion expression does not diverge physically at threshold.","formula":"\\[\\frac{g_m}{I_D}=\\begin{cases}2/V_{OV}&\\text{square-law strong inversion}\\\\1/(nU_T)&\\text{weak inversion}\\end{cases}\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":49,"page":31,"lecture":4,"lectureName":"Performance Measures of a MOS","topic":"Subthreshold swing","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"C","questionImage":"assets/questions/49-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/49-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 49\nL4 Performance Measures of a MOS · Subthreshold swing · Medium\nA g /I simulation in weak inversion gives n ≈ 1.52 at 300 K (U ≈ 25.85 mV). The subthreshold swing is\nm D T\napproximately:\nA. 39 mV/dec\nB. 60 mV/dec\nC. 90 mV/dec\nD. 120 mV/dec\n","summary":"The slope factor multiplies the ideal room-temperature swing, giving about 90 mV per decade.","steps":["In weak inversion, \\(\\ln I_D\\) changes by \\(\\Delta V_{GS}/(nU_T)\\). A tenfold current increase means \\(\\Delta\\ln I_D=\\ln10\\), so the required gate-voltage change is \\(SS=nU_T\\ln10\\).","Use the thermal voltage supplied: \\(1.52\\times25.85\\times2.3026\\approx90.5\\). Because \\(U_T\\) was entered in millivolts, the result is \\(90.5\\,\\mathrm{mV/dec}\\), closest to C.","The ideal \\(n=1\\) result is about \\(59.5\\,\\mathrm{mV/dec}\\). A larger slope factor reflects weaker gate control of the channel potential and requires a larger voltage change for each current decade."],"prerequisites":["Subthreshold exponential current","Base-10 versus natural logarithms","Thermal voltage and non-ideality factor"],"pitfall":"\\(nU_T\\approx39\\,\\mathrm{mV}\\) is the change for an e-fold, not a decade. The missing factor would be \\(\\ln10\\).","formula":"\\[SS=nU_T\\ln10=1.52(25.85\\,\\mathrm{mV})(2.3026)\\approx90.5\\,\\mathrm{mV/dec}\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":50,"page":31,"lecture":4,"lectureName":"Performance Measures of a MOS","topic":"Current density","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"D","questionImage":"assets/questions/50-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/50-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 50\nL4 Performance Measures of a MOS · Current density · Medium\nAt fixed I and L, reducing the width W (raising the current density I /W):\nD D\nA. Raises g /I and g , and lowers f\nm D m T\nB. Leaves V and g unchanged, since I is fixed\nOV m D\nC. Raises g and f , because g ∝ 1/W\nm T m\nD. Lowers g /I and g , reduces C , and generally raises f\nm D m gg T\n","summary":"At fixed current, a narrower transistor sacrifices transconductance efficiency but reduces capacitance enough to improve fT.","steps":["Keeping \\(I_D\\) fixed while reducing \\(W\\) reduces \\(K=\\mu C_{ox}W/L\\). To maintain the same current, the required overdrive rises: \\(V_{OV}=\\sqrt{2I_DL/(\\mu C_{ox}W)}\\).","Because \\(g_m=2I_D/V_{OV}\\), both \\(g_m\\) and \\(g_m/I_D\\) fall. Equivalently, at fixed current and length, \\(g_m\\propto\\sqrt W\\).","The intrinsic gate capacitance is proportional to width, \\(C_{gg}\\propto W\\). It therefore falls faster than \\(g_m\\), and \\(f_T=g_m/(2\\pi C_{gg})\\propto1/\\sqrt W\\) rises. Option D captures all three effects."],"prerequisites":["Square-law current at fixed ID","Current density ID/W","Transit frequency as gm divided by capacitance"],"pitfall":"Keeping current fixed does not keep overdrive or transconductance fixed. Always identify what is held constant before applying scaling laws.","formula":"\\[V_{OV}\\propto W^{-1/2},\\qquad g_m\\propto W^{1/2},\\qquad C_{gg}\\propto W,\\qquad f_T\\propto W^{-1/2}\\]","sourceNote":"The stated scaling assumes fixed length and the ideal long-channel capacitance model; practical parasitics can change the trend."},{"id":51,"page":32,"lecture":4,"lectureName":"Performance Measures of a MOS","topic":"gm/ID sizing","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"A","questionImage":"assets/questions/51-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/51-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 51\nL4 Performance Measures of a MOS · gm/ID sizing · Medium\nThe practical g /I sizing sequence is:\nm D\nA. Required g → choose g /I and L → I = g /(g /I ) → W = I /(I /W), reading I /W from the device\nm m D D m m D D D D\ntable\nB. Choose W and L, compute I from the square law, then read g from the table\nD m\nC. Choose V , size W from the square law and adjust it in simulation until the gain is met\nOV\nD. Choose the minimum L and W, then adjust V until the gain and bandwidth are met\nGS\n","summary":"Start with the required transconductance, then use efficiency and current-density tables to determine current and width.","steps":["Circuit requirements such as gain, bandwidth, or noise establish a required \\(g_m\\). Choose a channel length and a target \\(g_m/I_D\\) that balance intrinsic gain, speed, and current efficiency.","The current follows directly from the chosen efficiency: \\(I_D=g_m/(g_m/I_D)\\). For example, \\(g_m=1\\,\\mathrm{mS}\\) and \\(g_m/I_D=10\\,\\mathrm{V}^{-1}\\) imply \\(I_D=100\\,\\mu\\mathrm{A}\\).","At the selected operating point and length, read the simulated current density \\(J=I_D/W\\). Then \\(W=I_D/J\\); if \\(J=10\\,\\mu\\mathrm{A}/\\mu\\mathrm{m}\\), the illustrative current above requires \\(W=10\\,\\mu\\mathrm{m}\\).","Finally verify the resulting gain, capacitance, speed, and voltage headroom. Device tables capture real model behavior across inversion levels; simply guessing minimum dimensions or relying exclusively on the square law does not implement this sizing method."],"prerequisites":["Transconductance from circuit specifications","gm/ID as current efficiency","Current density and device lookup tables"],"pitfall":"\\(g_m/I_D\\) determines current, but it does not determine width without \\(I_D/W\\).","formula":"\\[I_D=\\frac{g_m}{g_m/I_D},\\qquad W=\\frac{I_D}{I_D/W}\\]","sourceNote":"The numerical example in the explanation is illustrative; the original question asks for the design sequence."},{"id":52,"page":32,"lecture":4,"lectureName":"Performance Measures of a MOS","topic":"I–V curves","difficulty":"Medium","options":["V","A","B","C","D"],"answer":"A","questionImage":"assets/questions/52-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/52-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 52\nL4 Performance Measures of a MOS · I–V curves · Medium\nThe figure shows log(I ) versus V of a MOSFET in weak inversion: I = 1 nA at V = 0.10 V and 1 μA at V =\nD GS D GS GS\n0.34 V. The subthreshold swing and the non-ideality factor n (300 K) are approximately:\nA. 80 mV/dec, n ≈ 1.35\nB. 60 mV/dec, n ≈ 1.0\nC. 120 mV/dec, n ≈ 2.0\nD. 240 mV/dec, n ≈ 4.0\n","summary":"A 0.24 V gate change produces three current decades, so the swing is 80 mV/dec and n is about 1.35.","steps":["Compute the current ratio before counting decades: \\(1\\,\\mu\\mathrm{A}/1\\,\\mathrm{nA}=1000=10^3\\). The plot therefore spans three decades, not one.","The voltage change is \\(0.34-0.10=0.24\\,\\mathrm{V}=240\\,\\mathrm{mV}\\). Divide by the number of decades: \\(SS=240/3=80\\,\\mathrm{mV/dec}\\).","Use \\(n=SS/(U_T\\ln10)\\). The ideal room-temperature denominator is approximately \\(59.5\\text{--}59.9\\,\\mathrm{mV/dec}\\), giving \\(n\\approx1.34\\text{--}1.35\\), which matches option A."],"prerequisites":["Logarithmic current axes","SI prefixes nano and micro","Relation between swing and slope factor"],"pitfall":"The full 240 mV span covers three decades. Also distinguish \\(\\log_{10}\\) in reading the graph from \\(\\ln\\) in the exponential model.","formula":"\\[SS=\\frac{\\Delta V_{GS}}{\\log_{10}(I_2/I_1)}=\\frac{240}{3}=80\\,\\mathrm{mV/dec},\\qquad n\\approx\\frac{80}{59.5}=1.35\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":53,"page":33,"lecture":4,"lectureName":"Performance Measures of a MOS","topic":"Intrinsic gain","difficulty":"Medium","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"D","questionImage":"assets/questions/53-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/53-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 53\nL4 Performance Measures of a MOS · Intrinsic gain · Medium\nBoth the width and the length of a MOSFET are doubled (figure) while V = V is kept constant. Its\nDSAT OV\nintrinsic gain g r :\nm o\nA. Is unchanged, because the aspect ratio W/L and the overdrive are the same\nB. Roughly halves, because the larger device carries more current and r falls\no\nC. Quadruples, because both g and r double\nm o\nD. Roughly doubles: W/L and therefore I and g are unchanged, but λ ∝ 1/L halves, so r = 1/(λI )\nD m o D\ndoubles\n","summary":"Doubling both dimensions preserves current and transconductance but roughly doubles output resistance and intrinsic gain.","steps":["Both dimensions change by the same factor, so \\(W/L\\) is unchanged. With the same \\(V_{OV}\\), the leading square-law current \\(I_D=\\tfrac12\\mu C_{ox}(W/L)V_{OV}^2\\) and \\(g_m=\\mu C_{ox}(W/L)V_{OV}\\) remain the same.","Channel-length modulation weakens in a longer channel. With the approximation \\(\\lambda\\propto1/L\\), doubling \\(L\\) halves \\(\\lambda\\). Thus \\(r_o\\approx1/(\\lambda I_D)\\) doubles at unchanged current.","Intrinsic gain is \\(g_mr_o\\), so it doubles because only \\(r_o\\) doubles. The gate area \\(WL\\) quadruples, increasing intrinsic capacitance by four; with unchanged \\(g_m\\), \\(f_T\\) falls by approximately four."],"prerequisites":["Aspect ratio versus absolute dimensions","Channel-length modulation scaling","Intrinsic gain and gate-area capacitance"],"pitfall":"Equal \\(W/L\\) preserves the ideal current but does not preserve channel-length modulation or capacitance.","formula":"\\[\\frac{W'}{L'}=\\frac WL,\\quad g_m'\\approx g_m,\\quad r_o'\\approx2r_o,\\quad (g_mr_o)'\\approx2g_mr_o\\]","sourceNote":"The gain scaling is approximate and uses the question bank's long-channel assumption that lambda is inversely proportional to channel length."},{"id":54,"page":34,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"Common-gate stage","difficulty":"Hard","options":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":"E","questionImage":"assets/questions/54-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/54-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 54\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · Common-gate stage · Hard\nFor a common-gate stage with drain load R and finite r (gate at AC ground, body effect neglected), the\nD o\nexact input resistance looking into the source is:\nA. 1/g\nm\nB. (1 + g r )/(r + R )\nm o o D\nC. r ∥ R\no D\nD. R /(g r )\nD m o\nE. (r + R )/(1 + g r )\no D m o\n","summary":"Finite output resistance couples the drain load back to the source, giving (ro + RD)/(1 + gm ro).","steps":["Apply a test voltage \\(v_x\\) at the source with the gate at AC ground. Then \\(v_{gs}=-v_x\\). Let \\(i_x\\) be the current entering the transistor from the test source.","The current entering the source is \\(i_x=g_mv_x+(v_x-v_d)/r_o\\): one contribution comes from transconductance and one from the output resistor. The same current leaves the drain through \\(R_D\\), so \\(v_d=i_xR_D\\).","Substitute the drain voltage: \\(i_x=g_mv_x+(v_x-i_xR_D)/r_o\\). Rearranging gives \\(i_x(r_o+R_D)=v_x(1+g_mr_o)\\), hence \\(R_{in}=v_x/i_x=(r_o+R_D)/(1+g_mr_o)\\).","If \\(g_mr_o\\gg1\\) and \\(r_o\\gg R_D\\), the familiar approximation \\(R_{in}\\approx1/g_m\\) follows. Option A omits the finite-load correction; option B is the reciprocal and has conductance units."],"prerequisites":["Small-signal MOS model with finite ro","Test-source method for input resistance","KCL and common-gate sign conventions"],"pitfall":"\\(1/g_m\\) is an approximation. Checking units immediately rejects option B as a resistance.","formula":"\\[R_{in}=\\frac{r_o+R_D}{1+g_mr_o}\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":55,"page":35,"lecture":1,"lectureName":"Prerequisites Recap","topic":"I–V curves","difficulty":"Hard","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"B","questionImage":"assets/questions/55-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/55-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 55\nL1 Prerequisites Recap · I–V curves · Hard\nA common-source stage with R = 5 kΩ and V = 1.8 V uses the NMOS whose output characteristics are\nD DD\nshown (V = 0.4 V, K = 2 mA/V², λ = 0); the load line is drawn. With the gate biased at V = 0.8 V, the\nTH GS\nquiescent V and the small-signal voltage gain are approximately:\nDS\nA. V ≈ 1.0 V, A ≈ −8\nDS v\nB. V ≈ 1.0 V, A ≈ −4\nDS v\nC. V ≈ 0.8 V, A ≈ −4\nDS v\nD. V ≈ 1.4 V, A ≈ −2\nDS v\n","summary":"The load-line bias is VDS = 1.0 V, and the common-source small-signal gain is -4.","steps":["Find the overdrive: \\(V_{OV}=V_{GS}-V_{TH}=0.8-0.4=0.4\\,\\mathrm V\\). Assuming saturation, \\(I_D=\\tfrac12 K V_{OV}^2=\\tfrac12(2\\,\\mathrm{mA/V^2})(0.4\\,\\mathrm V)^2=0.16\\,\\mathrm{mA}\\).","Apply the load line: \\(V_{DS}=V_{DD}-I_DR_D=1.8-(0.16\\,\\mathrm{mA})(5\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega})=1.0\\,\\mathrm V\\). Check the assumption: \\(1.0\\,\\mathrm V>V_{OV}=0.4\\,\\mathrm V\\), so the operating point is in saturation.","The small-signal transconductance is \\(g_m=KV_{OV}=0.8\\,\\mathrm{mS}\\). Since \\(\\lambda=0\\), \\(r_o\\to\\infty\\), and the unloaded common-source gain is \\(A_v=-g_mR_D=-(0.8\\,\\mathrm{mS})(5\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega})=-4\\)."],"prerequisites":["Square-law saturation current and gm","Resistive load line","Saturation check and common-source inversion"],"pitfall":"The factor one half belongs in the square-law current, but not in \\(g_m=KV_{OV}\\). Do not confuse \\(V_{GS}\\) with \\(V_{OV}\\).","formula":"\\[I_D=0.16\\,\\mathrm{mA},\\qquad V_{DS}=1.0\\,\\mathrm V,\\qquad A_v=-4\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":56,"page":35,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Body effect","difficulty":"Hard","options":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":"D","questionImage":"assets/questions/56-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/56-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 56\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Body effect · Hard\nAn NMOS has V = 0.3 V, γ = 0.4 V½ and 2Φ = 0.7 V. Its gate is at 1.2 V and its source at ground, while the\nTH0 F\nbulk is driven to a negative voltage V . The device turns off once V reaches 1.2 V, which happens for V\nX TH X\nbelow approximately:\nA. −0.7 V\nB. −1.2 V\nC. −3.1 V\nD. −8.8 V\nE. −12 V\n","summary":"The body-effect equation requires approximately -8.8 V of bulk bias to raise the model threshold to 1.2 V.","steps":["The source is grounded and the bulk is at \\(V_X\\), so \\(V_{SB}=V_S-V_B=-V_X\\). The threshold is \\(V_{TH}=V_{TH0}+\\gamma(\\sqrt{2\\Phi_F+V_{SB}}-\\sqrt{2\\Phi_F})\\). A more negative bulk therefore raises the NMOS threshold.","At the specified turn-off boundary, set \\(V_{TH}=V_{GS}=1.2\\,\\mathrm V\\). Substitute the given parameters: \\(1.2=0.3+0.4(\\sqrt{0.7-V_X}-\\sqrt{0.7})\\).","Isolate the square root: \\(\\sqrt{0.7-V_X}=0.9/0.4+\\sqrt{0.7}\\approx3.0867\\). Squaring yields \\(0.7-V_X\\approx9.5275\\), so \\(V_X\\approx-8.83\\,\\mathrm V\\), closest to D."],"prerequisites":["Body-effect threshold equation","Sign of VSB for grounded source and negative bulk","Algebra with square roots"],"pitfall":"The bias inside the root is \\(V_{SB}=-V_X\\), not \\(V_X\\). The square-root dependence makes body control much weaker than gate control.","formula":"\\[V_X=2\\Phi_F-\\left(\\frac{V_{GS}-V_{TH0}}{\\gamma}+\\sqrt{2\\Phi_F}\\right)^2\\approx-8.8\\,\\mathrm V\\]","sourceNote":"This is the threshold boundary in the stated body-effect model. Real devices retain subthreshold current and may not tolerate such a large body bias; the calculation is not an operating-voltage recommendation."},{"id":57,"page":36,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Body effect","difficulty":"Hard","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"C","questionImage":"assets/questions/57-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/57-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 57\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Body effect · Hard\nA source follower is biased by an ideal current source I , with the NMOS bulk at ground. As V rises, what\n1 in\ndoes the body effect do?\nA. Nothing: V is fixed by the process, so V tracks V with a constant V shift\nTH out in GS\nB. V falls, so V falls and V rises more than V (gain above 1)\nSB TH out in\nC. V rises, so V rises and V = V − V must grow to keep I = I : the gain falls below 1\nSB TH GS in out D 1\nD. It matters only in triode, since in saturation the drain current does not depend on V\nTH\n","summary":"As the source rises above the grounded bulk, threshold rises, requiring a larger gate-source voltage and reducing follower gain.","steps":["The ideal load fixes \\(I_D=I_1\\). Neglecting channel-length modulation, the required overdrive \\(V_{OV}\\) stays approximately constant because the device geometry and current are fixed.","When the output/source rises, \\(V_{SB}=V_{out}\\) increases. The body effect raises \\(V_{TH}\\). Maintaining the same overdrive therefore requires a larger \\(V_{GS}=V_{TH}+V_{OV}\\).","Because \\(V_{in}=V_{out}+V_{GS}(V_{out})\\), an increase in input must supply both the source rise and the extra threshold-related gate-source voltage. The output increment is smaller than the input increment.","In small-signal form, constant current gives \\(0=g_m(v_{in}-v_{out})-g_{mb}v_{out}\\). Thus \\(A_v=g_m/(g_m+g_{mb})=1/(1+\\eta)<1\\), where \\(\\eta=g_{mb}/g_m\\)."],"prerequisites":["Source-follower DC level shift","Body effect and body transconductance","Ideal current-source incremental resistance"],"pitfall":"Fixed drain current fixes overdrive approximately, not gate-source voltage, when the threshold changes.","formula":"\\[A_v=\\frac{g_m}{g_m+g_{mb}}=\\frac{1}{1+\\eta}<1\\]","sourceNote":"The stated gain neglects transistor output conductance and assumes an ideal current-source load, as in the published solution."},{"id":58,"page":37,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Temperature dependence","difficulty":"Hard","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"B","questionImage":"assets/questions/58-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/58-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 58\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Temperature dependence · Hard\nAn NMOS with V = 0.4 V is biased at V = 1.0 V, i.e. V = 0.6 V, below the zero-temperature-coefficient\nTH GS OV\noverdrive V ≈ 0.8 V (figure), so its current rises with temperature. Keeping the same drain current,\nOV,ZTC\nwhich change gives I a negative temperature coefficient?\nD\nA. Double W/L and re-bias: the same current then needs a smaller V , which moves the device below\nOV\nV\nOV,ZTC\nB. Halve W/L and re-bias: the same current then needs V ≈ 0.85 V, above V , where the mobility\nOV OV,ZTC\nterm dominates\nC. Double both W and L: W/L and V are unchanged, so the temperature coefficient becomes\nOV\nnegative through the longer channel\nD. Nothing can be done by sizing, because V itself scales with W/L\nOV,ZTC\n","summary":"Halving W/L raises the required overdrive to about 0.85 V, above the zero-temperature-coefficient point.","steps":["Two temperature effects compete at fixed gate bias: mobility decreases with temperature, reducing current, while threshold voltage decreases, increasing overdrive and current. The threshold effect dominates below the stated \\(V_{OV,ZTC}\\approx0.8\\,\\mathrm V\\); mobility dominates above it.","At the reference temperature, maintaining the same current gives \\(V_{OV}=\\sqrt{2I_D/K}\\), with \\(K\\propto W/L\\). Halving \\(W/L\\) therefore multiplies the required overdrive by \\(\\sqrt2\\).","The new overdrive is \\(0.6\\sqrt2\\approx0.849\\,\\mathrm V\\), just above \\(0.8\\,\\mathrm V\\). After re-biasing to this operating point, current decreases as temperature rises under the stated temperature model.","Doubling \\(W/L\\) instead reduces overdrive to about \\(0.424\\,\\mathrm V\\), strengthening the positive coefficient. Doubling both dimensions leaves the ratio and overdrive unchanged."],"prerequisites":["Temperature dependence of mobility and threshold","Zero-temperature-coefficient operating point","Square-law resizing at fixed nominal current"],"pitfall":"The same current is restored at the reference temperature by re-biasing. It is not actively held fixed during the temperature sweep; otherwise a current temperature coefficient would not be measured.","formula":"\\[V_{OV,new}=V_{OV,old}\\sqrt{\\frac{(W/L)_{old}}{(W/L)_{new}}}=0.6\\sqrt2\\approx0.85\\,\\mathrm V\\]","sourceNote":null},{"id":59,"page":38,"lecture":3,"lectureName":"Transistor Impairments","topic":"Velocity saturation","difficulty":"Hard","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"C","questionImage":"assets/questions/59-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/59-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 59\nL3 Transistor Impairments · Velocity saturation · Hard\nWith velocity saturation, I = K[V V − ½V ²] / (1 + V /(E L)), with V = V − V and K = μC W/L.\nD GT DS DS DS c GT GS TH ox\nCompared with the square law, the I –V curves show:\nD DS\nA. Higher saturation current at high overdrive, with V moving to a higher voltage\nDSAT\nB. The largest penalty at low overdrive, where the lateral field is highest, and none at high overdrive\nC. Lower saturation current at high overdrive (960 → 600 μA at V = 1.2 V) and V moving left (0.9\nGS DSAT\n→ 0.56 V)\nD. I independent of L, since the denominator cancels the 1/L in K\nD\n","summary":"Velocity saturation suppresses high-field current and moves the onset of saturation to a smaller drain voltage.","steps":["The numerator is the ordinary triode current. The denominator \\(1+V_{DS}/(E_cL)\\) exceeds one and accounts for the loss of proportionality between drift velocity and lateral electric field. The effect becomes stronger for larger drain voltage or shorter channel length.","At low overdrive the relevant drain voltages and fields are small, so the denominator remains close to one and the curves nearly match the square law. At high overdrive the correction is substantial: the quoted saturation current decreases from \\(960\\) to \\(600\\,\\mu\\mathrm A\\).","Differentiating the given expression with respect to \\(V_{DS}\\) locates the maximum at \\(V_{DSAT}=E_cL(\\sqrt{1+2V_{GT}/(E_cL)}-1)\\), which is smaller than \\(V_{GT}\\). This explains the quoted leftward shift from \\(0.9\\) to \\(0.56\\,\\mathrm V\\).","In the strong velocity-saturation limit, current becomes approximately linear in overdrive and \\(g_m\\) approaches the scale \\(WC_{ox}v_{sat}\\). The full expression still depends on channel length, so option D is not generally valid."],"prerequisites":["Lateral electric field and carrier velocity saturation","Square-law pinch-off boundary","Effect of short channel length on high-field transport"],"pitfall":"Velocity saturation limits carrier transport; it does not increase the saturation current. Do not confuse it with the ideal long-channel pinch-off mechanism.","formula":"\\[V_{DSAT}=E_cL\\left(\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2V_{GT}}{E_cL}}-1\\right)<V_{GT}\\]","sourceNote":"The particular 960-to-600 microamp and 0.9-to-0.56 volt values are the lecture example reproduced in the question bank."},{"id":60,"page":38,"lecture":4,"lectureName":"Performance Measures of a MOS","topic":"Figures of merit","difficulty":"Hard","options":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":"E","questionImage":"assets/questions/60-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/60-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 60\nL4 Performance Measures of a MOS · Figures of merit · Hard\nIn strong inversion, at fixed V , the channel length is halved. How do g /I , g /C and their product\nOV m D m gs\nchange?\nA. g /I unchanged, g /C ×2, product ×2\nm D m gs\nB. g /I ×2, g /C ×2, product ×4\nm D m gs\nC. All three unchanged, since only V sets the trade-off\nOV\nD. g /I ×½, g /C ×4, product ×2\nm D m gs\nE. g /I unchanged, g /C ×4, product ×4\nm D m gs\n","summary":"At fixed overdrive, halving length preserves gm/ID but quadruples gm/Cgs and their product.","steps":["In square-law strong inversion, \\(g_m/I_D=2/V_{OV}\\). Since overdrive stays fixed and this expression contains no length, the efficiency ratio is unchanged.","Using the long-channel saturation capacitance gives \\(g_m/C_{gs}=\\tfrac32\\mu V_{OV}/L^2\\). Replacing \\(L\\) by \\(L/2\\) multiplies this quantity by \\(1/(1/2)^2=4\\).","The product is \\((g_m/I_D)(g_m/C_{gs})=3\\mu/L^2\\). It also quadruples. Thus the factors are \\(1,4,4\\), exactly option E."],"prerequisites":["Strong-inversion transconductance efficiency","Intrinsic saturation gate capacitance","Inverse-square scaling with channel length"],"pitfall":"At fixed overdrive, shortening length improves the speed metric and the product; it does not improve \\(g_m/I_D\\).","formula":"\\[\\frac{g_m}{I_D}=\\frac2{V_{OV}},\\qquad\\frac{g_m}{C_{gs}}=\\frac{3\\mu V_{OV}}{2L^2},\\qquad\\frac{g_m}{I_D}\\frac{g_m}{C_{gs}}=\\frac{3\\mu}{L^2}\\]","sourceNote":"The final phrase of the published explanation says a shorter length “improves both.” Under the question's fixed-overdrive condition, the precise result is that gm/ID is unchanged; speed and the product improve."},{"id":61,"page":39,"lecture":4,"lectureName":"Performance Measures of a MOS","topic":"gm/ID design","difficulty":"Hard","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"A","questionImage":"assets/questions/61-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/61-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 61\nL4 Performance Measures of a MOS · gm/ID design · Hard\nIn the lecture's common-source design example (C = 1 pF, |A | ≥ 30, BW = 2.5 MHz, L = 0.5 μm), the\nload v\ncandidates g /I = 5, 12 and 20 V−1 were compared. g /I ≈ 12 was chosen because:\nm D m D\nA. 5 fails the gain spec and burns the most current; 20 makes W and C large with f only about 8×\ngg T\nabove the GBW; 12 meets the gain with margin at moderate current\nB. It gives the highest f (about 7 GHz) and the smallest device, so it is the fastest of the three\nT\ncandidates\nC. It gives the lowest current (about 31 μA) and the smallest C , so it is the most power-efficient of the\ngg\nthree\nD. The device table at L = 0.5 μm was characterized only at g /I = 12, so no other value could be used\nm D\n","summary":"The moderate-inversion choice meets the gain target while keeping current and device capacitance reasonable.","steps":["Compare the supplied device-table results at \\(g_m/I_D=5,12,20\\,\\mathrm{V}^{-1}\\). The intrinsic gains are approximately \\(25,40,45\\). The \\(5\\,\\mathrm{V}^{-1}\\) choice fails the required gain of 30 and uses the largest current, about \\(126\\,\\mu\\mathrm A\\), even though it is fastest.","The \\(20\\,\\mathrm{V}^{-1}\\) choice uses only about \\(31\\,\\mu\\mathrm A\\), but needs a much wider device: about \\(21\\,\\mu\\mathrm m\\), with \\(C_{gg}\\approx120\\,\\mathrm{fF}\\) and \\(f_T\\approx0.8\\,\\mathrm{GHz}\\). High current efficiency comes with greater capacitance and less speed margin.","The selected \\(12\\,\\mathrm{V}^{-1}\\) point gives gain about 40, current about \\(52\\,\\mu\\mathrm A\\), width about \\(6.5\\,\\mu\\mathrm m\\), and \\(C_{gg}\\approx37\\,\\mathrm{fF}\\). Its \\(f_T\\approx2.7\\,\\mathrm{GHz}\\) leaves much more room above the design's bandwidth requirement.","The bank compares speed to \\(GBW=40\\times2.5\\,\\mathrm{MHz}=100\\,\\mathrm{MHz}\\), using the achieved gain. The speed margins are therefore about \\(27\\) for the selected point and \\(8\\) for the high-efficiency point. The choice balances requirements rather than maximizing a single metric."],"prerequisites":["gm/ID lookup-table design","Intrinsic gain, bandwidth, and gain-bandwidth product","Trade-offs between current efficiency and capacitance"],"pitfall":"The highest \\(f_T\\), lowest current, and best overall design are not necessarily the same candidate. Option B describes the low-efficiency candidate's speed, not the selected point.","formula":"\\[GBW=40(2.5\\,\\mathrm{MHz})=100\\,\\mathrm{MHz},\\qquad\\frac{2.7\\,\\mathrm{GHz}}{GBW}=27\\]","sourceNote":"The numerical device characteristics are lecture-table values supplied by the published answer, not independently derived from the few specifications in the question. The 27x and 8x comparisons use the bank's common 100 MHz reference."},{"id":62,"page":39,"lecture":4,"lectureName":"Performance Measures of a MOS","topic":"Current density","difficulty":"Hard","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"B","questionImage":"assets/questions/62-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/62-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 62\nL4 Performance Measures of a MOS · Current density · Hard\nAt fixed drain current I (square law, saturation), how does f scale with the width W?\nD T\nA. g ∝ W and C ∝ W, so f is independent of W\nm gs T\nB. g ∝ √W while C ∝ W, so f ∝ 1/√W: a narrower device is faster\nm gs T\nC. g ∝ W while C ∝ √W, so f ∝ √W: a wider device is faster\nm gs T\nD. g ∝ 1/W and C ∝ W, so f ∝ 1/W²\nm gs T\n","summary":"At fixed current and length, gm scales as sqrt(W), capacitance scales as W, and fT scales as 1/sqrt(W).","steps":["Eliminate overdrive from the square-law equations to obtain \\(g_m=\\sqrt{2\\mu C_{ox}(W/L)I_D}\\). With \\(I_D\\), \\(L\\), and process parameters fixed, \\(g_m\\propto\\sqrt W\\).","The intrinsic saturation gate-source capacitance is \\(C_{gs}=\\tfrac23 WLC_{ox}\\), so it is proportional to \\(W\\). Combining the two gives \\(f_T=g_m/(2\\pi C_{gs})\\propto\\sqrt W/W=1/\\sqrt W\\).","For example, reducing width by a factor of four halves \\(g_m\\), quarters \\(C_{gs}\\), and doubles \\(f_T\\). The narrower device must operate at higher overdrive to carry the fixed current, so the increased speed costs transconductance efficiency."],"prerequisites":["gm expressed in terms of drain current","Width dependence of gate capacitance","Fixed-current versus fixed-overdrive scaling"],"pitfall":"The statement \\(g_m\\propto W\\) applies at fixed overdrive, not at fixed current. Changing which bias quantity is held fixed changes the answer.","formula":"\\[g_m\\propto\\sqrt W,\\qquad C_{gs}\\propto W,\\qquad f_T\\propto W^{-1/2}\\]","sourceNote":"Length and process parameters are implicitly held fixed, and the result uses the stated long-channel square-law model."},{"id":63,"page":40,"lecture":4,"lectureName":"Performance Measures of a MOS","topic":"Subthreshold swing","difficulty":"Hard","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":"D","questionImage":"assets/questions/63-question.webp","answerImage":"assets/questions/63-answer.webp","searchText":"Question 63\nL4 Performance Measures of a MOS · Subthreshold swing · Hard\nThe GPDK090 PMOS model reports a subthreshold swing of 57–59.3 mV/dec for L ≤ 0.2 μm, improving as\nthe channel shortens. The correct interpretation is:\nA. An excellent short-channel PMOS, whose electrostatics improve as L shrinks and whose n\napproaches 1\nB. Evidence that holes have a higher mobility than electrons in this process, which lowers n below 1\nC. Proof that the NMOS model, which reports 94 mV/dec at L = 0.1 μm, is the one that is wrong\nD. A model artifact: with n ≥ 1, SS cannot fall below U ·ln10 ≈ 59–60 mV/dec at room temperature, and\nT\nreal short channels degrade SS\n","summary":"The reported subthermal swing and improvement with shorter channels indicate a model artifact within the conventional MOS assumptions.","steps":["For an ordinary thermionic MOSFET in the stated model, \\(SS=nU_T\\ln10\\), with \\(n=1+C_{dep}/C_{ox}\\ge1\\). At \\(300\\,\\mathrm K\\), the ideal lower limit is approximately \\(59.5\\,\\mathrm{mV/dec}\\).","A reported swing of \\(57\\,\\mathrm{mV/dec}\\) would require \\(n\\approx57/59.5\\approx0.96\\), contradicting the positive-capacitance expression. Values extremely close to the limit need care with temperature and numerical extraction, but the full trend in the question is inconsistent with this model.","Shortening a conventional channel generally weakens gate electrostatic control and increases the influence of drain and bulk fields. The reported systematic improvement as the channel shortens is therefore another reason to distrust this PMOS model's weak-inversion results.","Mobility changes the current scale and speed, but does not make \\(1+C_{dep}/C_{ox}\\) smaller than one. A plausible NMOS swing above the thermal limit is not evidence that the NMOS model is wrong. Option D identifies the modeling issue."],"prerequisites":["Subthreshold swing thermal limit","Slope factor from gate and depletion capacitances","Short-channel electrostatic degradation and model validation"],"pitfall":"Do not interpret an apparently better simulated number as better physics without checking the model's assumptions and physical bounds.","formula":"\\[SS=nU_T\\ln10\\ge U_T\\ln10\\approx59.5\\,\\mathrm{mV/dec}\\quad(T=300\\,\\mathrm K)\\]","sourceNote":"The thermal limit here applies to the conventional equilibrium thermionic MOS model used by this course. It is not a universal prohibition on specialized transistor mechanisms or different operating conditions."}];
